    47	- IN-17 The instruction must not pre-announce what the agent is supposed to discover. Withholding is the default and difficulty is the point: traps, misleading surfaces, data whose shape only emerges from reading it, and rules that show themselves under a single condition are all legitimate, and gate 02 DS-21 protects them. What fails is a prompt that hands over the input's hidden structure, or the branches the work requires, before the agent has looked: "watch out for the null timestamps", "note that two records share an id", "some rows still use the old format", "remember the fiscal year starts in April". Each sentence like that converts a discovery into a transcription, and the bundle still grades clean afterwards, because every announced branch gets its fixture and AL-27 confirms the fixture fires, so nothing downstream will catch it and this gate is the only place it can be caught. Two conditions have to hold together, and the second is the one that separates a hint from a requirement. First, the sentence announces something the agent was meant to find for itself: a property of the input, an edge case inside it, a branch the work has to take. Second, the shipped input ALREADY establishes that thing, so the agent could have read it off the data it was handed (gate 05 AL-1 calls such a fact determinate and carries the worked cases to measure against, the sharpest being that an input which is merely silent about something has established nothing). Only when both hold is the sentence doing nothing but saving the agent the work. If the input does not establish it, the sentence is the only ground the graded item has, and deleting it would not make the task harder, it would make a graded item unfair (AL-1) or the task impossible (DS-21). That sentence is a requirement and it stays. Difficulty is not the test here and neither is how much the sentence gives away, which is why no rule of engagement is ever a hint: nothing in a data file can establish a prohibition, so "change only the files under src/parser" and "leave the config untouched" are requirements by construction, and IN-15 with gate 04 JD-16 oblige the author to state them. The same follows for a signpost that the bundle's own material is not authoritative (Final_checklist CL-6), for whatever settles a graded ambiguity (IN-16), and for the output's required shape and literals wherever the shipped input does not carry them itself (gate 02 DS-25). Default to keeping the sentence. To fail this check you must do two things: name the discovery the sentence replaces, and point to where the shipped input already establishes it. If you cannot do both, the prompt is being helpful rather than broken, and it passes. Orientation that discloses nothing is fine as well ("start by reading the policy file"), and so is any statement of what the finished work must look like. What this check removes is advance warning about what the agent will find once it starts reading.
