     1	# Gate 02 - Data, Environment & Solution QC
     2	
     3	The files under review are `environment/` (Dockerfile + `data/`) and `solution/` (solve.sh, solve.py, TRUTH.md) of one input bundle. To judge them you also open, read-only, `instruction.md` (DS-3, DS-10, DS-19, DS-20, DS-21, DS-25), `task.toml` (DS-14, DS-25), `tests/test_output.py` with its weight registrations (DS-15, DS-22, DS-24, DS-25) and `tests/judge.toml` (DS-17, DS-22). Your findings are still about the data, the environment and the solution (TRUTH.md included); defects inside those other files belong to their own gates. If you were not given the bundle path, ask for it and stop.
     4	
     5	**Read TRUTH.md last, and only after you have formed your own DS-21 judgement.** TRUTH.md is the author's worked answer: it names the intended defect, the traps and the solution. Read first, it tells you what to see and you will see it, and a task that is actually unsolvable will read as solvable because the answer was handed to you. So work DS-1 through DS-21 from the agent-visible material on its own, write down what you concluded, and only then open TRUTH.md to check whether it agrees. Use it to verify, never to orient.
     6	
     7	There is no script for this gate alone; `structure_qc.py` already verified the tree, the environment/ layout, and dash sweep. Your job is semantic: is the data right for THIS task, does the container actually support the task, and does the oracle actually solve it.
     8	
     9	One check here does lean on a script number. DS-24 quotes the oracle-versus-verifier overlap that `tests_qc.py` reports (its oracle-overlap line). If you already have that output, read the number off it. If you do not, run `python3 tests_qc.py /path/to/bundle` and take it from there. Either way the number only tells you where to look; DS-24 is decided by reading the two files.
    10	
    11	## Mindset
    12	
    13	The data folder is the world the agent lives in. It must be necessary (task unsolvable without it, DS-2), referentially complete and truthful (everything the instruction points at is really there and the claims about it are true, DS-3), enough to actually solve the task (DS-21, the one that matters and the one DS-3 does NOT give you), and clean (nothing leaks the answer, DS-4). The Dockerfile decides solvability too: a single missing binary makes an otherwise perfect task score zero for every honest agent. The solution is the proof the task is solvable; treat it as evidence, not decoration.
    14	
    15	## environment/ rules
    16	
    17	- DS-1 All input lives in one or more input directories under environment/ (any names: data/, repo/, fixtures/...), each holding at least one file and each copied into the image by environment/Dockerfile; environment/ holds only the Dockerfile and those directories. The names and the split are the task's choice: judge only that each directory is a coherent unit of input (a repository, a corpus, a set of rule files), not that it is called data/. Script-owned, here for context.
    18	- DS-2 Necessary: deleting the input directories must make the task unsolvable, and so must deleting any single one of them. If the agent could complete the task without ever opening an input directory, that directory is residue - fail.
    19	- DS-3 Referentially complete and truthful: every data file the instruction references exists here with the exact name; every claim the instruction makes about the data (counts, formats, fields, examples) is true of the actual bytes. Open the files and verify, never infer from file name only. Note this is a much weaker property than the data being enough to solve the task, which is DS-21. A bundle can pass DS-3 cleanly and still be impossible.
    20	- DS-4 NO ANSWERS IN DATA - strict, no exceptions. Nothing in any environment/ input directory may contain, encode, or make trivially derivable the expected final output of the task. A `*.solution.*` file, an answer key, a "labelled" file whose labels are the graded output, expected-output examples that the tests grade against - all fail. (Known real case: cache-adversary-synthesis shipped example_1.solution.json - that was a confirmed leak.) What is NOT a leak: a document describing the format, schema, protocol or rules the agent must work to. Contract material tells the agent what shape the answer takes; it does not hand over the answer, and a prompt is allowed to point at such a file instead of restating it (IN-8). So do not report one merely for existing. Read it before deciding, because the line is about content and not about the file being called a specification: a worked example carrying the graded values, a table of expected outputs, a walkthrough that computes a graded result, or prose stating the answer to a case anything grades is a leak whatever the document is titled. Confirm the answer genuinely is not recoverable by reading it, then move on.
    21	- DS-5 No template residue (read every TODO/FIXME/XXX/PLACEHOLDER hit in context yourself; whatever the script reports on those markers is a lead, not a verdict): a partial spec with "(redacted)" sections, generic sample_NNN/field_NNN corpora, or any file that reads as boilerplate from another task is a fail. The known-bad inherited pattern is a `<TAG>-v1.0-DRAFT.md` spec with redacted sections - its presence is an automatic fail. Data must be curated for this task per the task guidelines.
    22	- DS-6 Data files must make sense for the task story: formats parse, sizes are reasonable, damaged files are damaged on purpose (and the instruction says so).
    23	- DS-7 No em/en dashes in text data files (script-checked bundle-wide; re-verify any file the script skipped as binary if it is actually text).
    24	
    25	## environment/Dockerfile rules
    26	
    27	- DS-8 Must never COPY tests/ or solution/ content into the agent image, in any form.
    28	- DS-9 Must copy every input directory into the image at the path the instruction uses (conventionally /app/<dir>, e.g. /app/data, /app/repo), and the instruction must use those paths consistently.
    29	- DS-10 Solvability: every tool, interpreter, package or library that the instruction or the reference solution needs must be installed by this Dockerfile. Compare the instruction's demands and solve.sh/solve.py imports line by line against what the image installs. A named-but-missing tool is a fail.
    30	- DS-11 Offline contract respected. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so this is not a value to read and compare: assume no network and check that every layer can live with it. Everything the agent needs must be baked in at build time, and any runtime fetch is a fail - `pip install`, `apt-get`, `npm i`, `git clone` or `curl` reached for inside the task flow, in the instruction, or in the ORACLE's own path. Pay particular attention to the oracle: a `solve.sh` that installs a dependency it needs will simply fail at grade time, and a bundle whose own reference solution cannot run offline is broken rather than merely fragile. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines govern the judge and the agent harness reaching their models; they are not this check's business and do not license anything in the task flow. If the task genuinely needs an outside endpoint to be solvable, report that as a design finding against the offline contract.
    31	- DS-12 No secrets, no host paths, no `--privileged` assumptions, nothing that only works on the author's machine.
    32	- DS-28 Every artifact directory is writable by the agent user. `[agent] user = "1000:1000"` runs the agent, and the oracle's `solve.sh`, as uid 1000, not root. The directory each declared artifact is written into must be owned by uid 1000 (or world-writable) in the image this Dockerfile builds, for example `RUN mkdir -p /app && chown 1000:1000 /app` before `COPY data /app/data`. COPY and ADD write root-owned files unless they carry `--chown`, so a `chown` placed before a COPY into the same directory does not cover it. A root-owned output directory makes the oracle die with `PermissionError` before it writes anything, and Step 10 reads 0.000. Fail. structure_qc S8 replays the Dockerfile's `mkdir`, `chown`, `chmod`, `COPY --chown`, `WORKDIR`, `USER` and `useradd -u` lines and checks the mechanics; you confirm anything it cannot see, such as ownership set by a script the Dockerfile runs, a base image's own users, or an `ENTRYPOINT` that changes permissions at start.
    33	- DS-29 The openhands-sdk agent runtime is pre-baked. The harness runs the agent through the openhands-sdk wrapper from `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv`, and it only installs that runtime itself, over the network, when the venv is missing. With `[environment] network_mode = "no-network"` that install cannot run, so the agent never starts. Every `environment/Dockerfile` must therefore carry, in its final stage: the system packages `curl`, `ca-certificates` and `tmux`; a venv at exactly `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv`; `openhands-sdk==1.55.1 openhands-tools==1.55.1 litellm==1.105.0 fastapi` installed into it (those exact pins; fastapi unpinned; litellm 1.106.0 fails to import alongside the mcp package openhands pulls in); a build-time `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv/bin/python -c "import openhands.sdk"` so a broken install fails the build; and `chmod -R a+rX /opt/openhands-sdk-venv` so uid 1000 can run it. The recommended block, matching the harness's own installer:
    34	
    35	  ```dockerfile
    36	  # anubis: pre-baked agent so a sealed (no-network) run can start the agent.
    37	  RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
    38	          curl ca-certificates tmux \
    39	      && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*
    40	  RUN curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh \
    41	      && . "$HOME/.local/bin/env" \
    42	      && uv venv /opt/openhands-sdk-venv --python /usr/local/bin/python3.12 --clear \
    43	      && . /opt/openhands-sdk-venv/bin/activate \
    44	      && uv pip install openhands-sdk==1.55.1 openhands-tools==1.55.1 litellm==1.105.0 fastapi \
    45	      && /opt/openhands-sdk-venv/bin/python -c "import openhands.sdk, openhands.tools" \
    46	      && chmod -R a+rX /opt/openhands-sdk-venv
    47	  ```
    48	
    49	  Any other install style that delivers every piece is equally correct and is not a finding, for example `python3.12 -m venv /opt/openhands-sdk-venv` followed by `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv/bin/pip install --no-cache-dir openhands-sdk==1.55.1 openhands-tools==1.55.1 litellm==1.105.0 fastapi`, or the apt packages folded into the image's main `apt-get install` line. Fail when a piece is missing or a pinned package carries another version or no version. structure_qc S9 checks each piece; you confirm the block sits in the final build stage and is not undone later (a later `rm`, a `chmod` that removes read access).
    50	- DS-30 The uid 1000 agent account exists. `[agent] user = "1000:1000"` runs the agent and the oracle as uid 1000, so the final stage of `environment/Dockerfile` must create that account with a home directory and point `HOME` at it; without them, tools that write under `$HOME` (uv, pip, the agent itself) fail. Put these right after `FROM`:
    51	
