    59	## solution/ rules
    60	
    61	- DS-13 solve.sh exists and is the entrypoint. solve.py is optional; if absent, solve.sh alone must carry the full solution.
    62	- DS-14 The oracle must genuinely do the work: read the inputs, compute, and write every declared artifact to the exact paths the instruction promises. An oracle that copies a prebaked answer into place without computing it is a fail (it proves nothing about solvability and hides broken tasks).
    63	- DS-15 Walk through solve.sh mentally against the tests: would it plausibly score 1.0? Every artifact produced, formats matching what tests expect, edge cases the tests grade actually handled. If you can name a test it would fail, that is a fail.
    64	- DS-16 The oracle MAY read environment/ (allowed - it runs in the agent container where the input directories exist).
    65	- DS-17 The oracle must never read or reference tests/ (test_output.py, judge.toml, reward.toml) - an oracle that peeks at the grader is a fail.
    66	- DS-18 Idempotency: running solve.sh twice should not corrupt the result - warn only.
    67	- DS-19 Stack conformance: solution code must respect constraints the contract imposes on the agent (stdlib-only, specific language, which files may be touched, which tool to use, what to leave alone). A rule of engagement counts when it is stated in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, so open those files too: a rule may live there rather than in the prompt. If the contract binds the agent to constraints the oracle itself violates, flag it.
    68	- DS-20 The oracle obeys the contract, independently of the tests. Passing every test (DS-15) is NOT proof: tests undercover, and an oracle can score 1.0 while violating a stated rule no fixture happens to trigger. Walk the oracle's logic against every rule the contract states, wherever that rule lives, including rules no test exercises. Ordered logic deserves special care: when the contract states a precedence or tie-break chain ("check A before B before C"), read the oracle's actual check order and compare it pairwise against the stated chain. The oracle is the ground truth of the task; when it disagrees with the contract, every agent that obeys the prompt gets punished for the oracle's bug. Any divergence between stated rule and oracle behaviour is a fail, even when no current test would catch it.
    69	- DS-24 The oracle is an independent implementation, not a copy of the verifier. Open `solution/solve.py` and `tests/test_output.py` side by side and judge whether the oracle solves the task or merely re-runs the grader's own code. tests_qc measures shared substantive lines, fails at 35% and warns from 20%, but the number is only a prompt to look: a small shared block can be the whole answer, and a large one can be incidental. This matters because a copied oracle is guaranteed to score 1.000. It cannot disagree with the grader, so the Step 10 oracle run returns a green light while proving nothing about whether the task is solvable at all. Two independent implementations only agree when both are right, and that agreement is the only evidence the oracle run actually produces. Some sharing is legitimate. A verifier that must generate a corpus needs an encoder, and the oracle needs one too. What is not legitimate is the oracle importing, copying or restating the logic that decides the grade. Ask which part of the shared code is the thing being graded: if the answer is "the shared part", the oracle proves nothing and this is a fail. Keep overlap as low as the task allows even when it is under the limit, and say so when you see an author sitting just beneath the line.
    70	- DS-21 Solvable from what the agent actually receives. This is the mirror of DS-2: DS-2 asks whether the data is needed, this asks whether it is enough. Close `solution/` and `tests/` in your head and hold only what the agent holds, which is `instruction.md` plus everything under `environment/`. Nothing else reaches the agent. One question: could a determined expert, given unlimited time and skill and nothing but those files, actually arrive at the graded answer? If yes, this check passes, however brutal the task is. Difficulty is the point and it may come from anywhere. Traps, deliberately misleading surfaces, rules that only show themselves under one specific condition, data scattered across many files, a value in one file that only makes sense once another file is understood, chains where each step unlocks the next, edge cases that punish a naive reading, long grinding search. None of that is a defect and none of it belongs in your report. Working it out is the agent's job, and a task that gives up its answer easily is a worse task. The defect is different in kind: information the agent needs that exists in none of its files and follows from nothing in them. The clearest case is a value the agent must reproduce exactly while nothing it can see constrains or confirms it, such as a secret key, a private label, or an arbitrary constant taken from the author's head. There the agent has nothing to reason from and no way to tell a right guess from a wrong one, so skill stops mattering and the task is impossible rather than hard. Default to pass. To fail this check you must do two things: name the exact piece of information the solver cannot obtain, and show that it appears in no agent-visible file and follows from nothing in them. If you cannot do both, the task is hard, not impossible, and it passes.
    71	- DS-25 The exact output shape is reachable. DS-21 asks whether the agent can work out the answer; this asks whether it can work out what the answer has to look like. They fail separately, and this one fails silently, because an agent can compute every value correctly and still score zero for emitting them under names it had to invent. Do it mechanically. List every literal the grading side requires of the agent's output: JSON key names, section headings, CSV column names, error strings, enum values, filenames the tests open, ordering the tests assume. Read them out of `tests/test_output.py` directly rather than trusting a summary, and include the ones built at runtime rather than written as constants, since string formatting hides them from any search. Then for each one, find where the agent could learn it: the instruction, a specification or schema file under `environment/`, or the shipped data itself, including the interiors of any archive the agent is given. Name the file for each. Any required literal that appears in no agent-visible file is a fail, because no amount of skill produces a name nobody supplied. Report the count and the specific names, for example "18 of 24 required keys appear in /app/data/SCHEMA.md, these 6 appear nowhere". A shape the agent must invent and the grader then insists on is the single most common way a solvable task becomes unpassable.
    72	- DS-26 Nothing that gives away the answer is reachable anywhere in the agent image. DS-4 covers `environment/`, which is where reviewers look. This covers everywhere else, because the agent has a whole filesystem and the instruction only points at part of it. Read `environment/Dockerfile` line by line and follow what each step leaves behind. Check for: a COPY or ADD that lands solution material outside /app/data, a RUN step that writes a worked answer, an expected output or a reference artifact into `/tmp`, `/root`, `/opt`, `/usr/local` or the image's working directory; a build stage that produces the answer and is then copied from; a package, wheel, git checkout or archive whose contents include the solution; a leftover script, log, cache or history file from building the broken state. Bind mounts and volumes count too. Fail on anything from which the answer can be recovered without doing the work, and say exactly where it lives and how the agent would reach it. The test is not whether the file looks like an answer; it is whether an agent that finds it can shortcut the task.
