    13	The script handles the mechanical layer (check/registration bijection, weight scale, counts and balance, reward.toml arithmetic, forbidden reads). Your job is the semantic layer: do these checks grade the right thing, fairly, and uncheatably.
    14	
    15	Throughout this gate a "test" means one rewardkit check in `test_output.py`: a top-level function decorated with `@criterion(description=...)`, taking only `workspace` and returning a bool, registered once at module level with `criteria.<name>(weight=W)`. rewardkit scores the module as the `test_output` input; `tests/reward.toml` combines it with the `judge` input into the one `reward`.
    16	
    17	## Mindset
    18	
    19	The tests ARE the task. An agent that satisfies the contract but fails the tests was robbed; an agent that games the tests without doing the work stole reward. Both are QC failures of the same root kind: the tests and the contract describe different tasks. A test is fair when what it reads is either promised by the instruction or determinate in the input the agent was handed, and the prompt does not have to point at the file that establishes it, since working out your own data is the task (TS-19). Read every test asking "what observable does this read, and could the agent have learned, from the prompt or from the input it was given, that this observable would matter?"
    20	
    21	## The two-container rule (memorize this)
    22	
    23	Grading runs in a fresh container built FROM `tests/Dockerfile` with `tests/` as the build context. No agent input path (`/app/data`, or wherever environment/Dockerfile COPYs an input directory) EXISTS there. Declared artifacts are uploaded to their exact declared absolute paths (e.g. `/app/decode.py`); the harness copies the declared path string verbatim, which is why task.toml artifacts must be absolute. Therefore:
    24	
    25	- TS-1 Nothing under tests/ may unconditionally read an agent input path (`/app/data` or any other input COPY destination) at grade time. Guaranteed crash. Fail. Comments and docstrings that merely mention the path are fine (the script skips them), and a read behind an existence check or env override is safe (the guard falls through to a fallback when the path is absent) - not flagged.
    26	- TS-2 `tests/Dockerfile` must `COPY . /tests/` (the image must own the test files: in separate mode Harbor 0.24.0 does not upload tests/ when tests/Dockerfile exists) and install exactly `harbor-rewardkit==0.2.1`, the version Harbor 0.24.0 pins. An unpinned, ranged or different version is a fail. The same line must pin litellm with `==` (the reference pin is `litellm==1.105.0`, what the agent image pins): rewardkit accepts `litellm>=1.83` with no upper bound, so an unpinned verifier takes whatever PyPI serves at build time, and a withdrawn litellm 1.107.0 stable shipped site-packages hooks that fetched and ran remote code in the container holding the judge key. Unpinned or a quarantined version is a fail; another exact version is a warn needing tasker approval.
    27	- TS-3 `tests/Dockerfile` should `RUN mkdir -p /app` (reference-task convention). The harness auto-creates artifact parent dirs at upload time, so absence is a warn, not a crash.
    28	- TS-4 `tests/Dockerfile` must install every third-party package test_output.py imports other than rewardkit (mind pip aliases: yaml/pyyaml, PIL/pillow, sklearn/scikit-learn) and every tool the scripts invoke (`util-linux` for `setpriv` or `runuser`), on Python 3.12 or newer, which rewardkit requires. pytest is not required. The agent image's packages are NOT available here.
    29	- TS-5 Any ground truth the tests need (expected values, fixtures, generators) must live inside tests/ itself, never be read from agent-writable paths.
    30	
    31	## test.sh rules
    32	
    33	- TS-6 rewardkit writes the reward; test.sh writes none. test.sh follows the template: it clears stale reward files (`reward.txt`, `reward.json`, `reward-details.json` under `/logs/verifier`), locks the log directory, restricts `/tests`, and its last line is `exec rewardkit /tests --workspace /app --output /logs/verifier/reward.json`. rewardkit then writes `reward.json` and `reward-details.json` beside it. test.sh writes no reward bytes itself (no `echo` or `printf` into a reward file, no Python that writes one), runs nothing after rewardkit, and has no branch that skips it. If rewardkit fails, no reward.json exists and Harbor records a verifier error; that is the intended outcome for an authoring defect, and Step 10 makes such a bundle unshippable. Bad agent output must never be what makes rewardkit fail: that is TS-29. Trusting a pre-existing reward file is a fail. The same verifier error is also the intended outcome of a real agent run with no `OPENAI_API_KEY` (rewardkit raises "Missing credentials") or with no `/logs/agent/trajectory.json` (rewardkit raises "Trajectory not found"): a run whose judged half was never scored has no reward to report, so neither is a finding and neither may be worked around in test.sh. Step 10 avoids both by running the tests-only copy `exe_copy.py` builds.
    34	- TS-7 Computes the reward exclusively from tests/ ground truth plus the uploaded artifacts. Never from anything the agent could have written outside the declared artifacts.
    35	- TS-8 No skip switches (`SKIP_TESTS=1`, `if [ -f /app/skip ]`, env-var bypasses).
    36	- TS-9 Never `source`/`exec`/import from agent-writable paths; not PATH-shadowable (does not rely on bare tool names resolvable to /app; absolute paths or controlled PATH).
    37	- TS-10 `tests/reward.toml` combines the channels correctly. It holds exactly one `[[reward]]` table with `name = "reward"`, `aggregation = "weighted-sum"` and `weights` naming exactly `test_output` and `judge`, plus one `[scoring.test_output]` table with `aggregation = "weighted-sum"`, and no other keys or tables. `weights.test_output` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive weights registered in test_output.py, and `weights.judge` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive criterion weights in judge.toml, both written at full float precision (Python's `repr` of the division). Recompute both from the actual files. The final reward is then half of (test weight earned / test positive total) plus half of (judge weight earned / judge positive total): 1 when every positive passes and no negative fires, 0 for doing nothing, -1 at worst. No clamp exists anywhere and none is needed, because neither channel's negatives may outweigh its positives (TS-23, gate 04 JD-12). A weight left stale after a check or criterion changed is a fail: the score then stops running from -1 to 1. No other top-level `.py` file under tests/ defines criteria, no other `.toml` carries `[judge]` or `[[criterion]]`, and no subdirectory of tests/ holds either, since rewardkit would score each one as an extra input.
    38	
    39	## test_output.py rules
    40	
    41	- TS-11 Deterministic: the same submission must earn the same score every run. Step 10 runs the oracle five times and requires 1.000 each time, but that only catches drift that happens to show up in five tries. Reading catches the rest, so walk the tests hunting for each of these by name: wall-clock or date values in an assertion, including "today" and elapsed-time bounds; unseeded `random` or `uuid`; iteration over a `set`, or over a `dict` built from a `set`; `glob`, `os.listdir`, `os.walk` or `Path.iterdir` consumed without sorting; tests that depend on running in a particular order or that mutate state a later test reads; timing margins tight enough to lose on a slow machine; locale, timezone or default-encoding dependence; hash-order effects; any network call at all. Because reward here is weighted rather than pass/fail, a single flaky check worth 3 of 53 points does not flip the verdict, it just moves the number a little on every run. That is invisible in one run and it is exactly the noise that ruins a training signal, since the agent is scored for something it did not do. Name the specific line, not the general worry.
    42	- TS-12 Outcome-driven: tests grade the final state and artifact behaviour, never the agent's command history or console output. This holds for stated rules of engagement too, and it decides which of them a test may own. In separate mode the verifier sees only the declared artifacts, so a test can grade scope or another effect only when the affected paths are declared artifacts; otherwise the judged channel owns it. A scope test that passes because something is absent is a negative (TS-25). A tool-use or ordering rule is not test material at all: proving it needs the command history, so the judged channel owns it, where the judge reads the trajectory (gate 04 JD-16). A test reaching into command history to check one is a fail even though the rule itself is legitimate. Where a test can grade a stated rule it should, since an assertion returns the same verdict every run and a judge does not, so a format, output-shape or declared-artifact scope rule left entirely to the judged channel is a real finding. Gate 04 JD-16 raises it, because deciding it needs the judged criteria in front of you; note it here if you spot it and leave the verdict to that gate.
    43	- TS-27 Reading the agent's source is not proof the agent did the work. Any assertion whose subject is the text of a file the agent wrote, rather than what that file does when run, grades appearance instead of behaviour. The shapes to look for are `assert "token" in source`, a regex over source text, a check that some identifier or import is present, a line count, or a diff size. Names like `x_was_repaired_not_bypassed` are a strong hint, since a check that has to claim it is not being fooled usually is. These pay out for typing the right words. An agent that writes a stub doing nothing and mentions the expected identifiers in a comment passes every one of them, which is not a hypothetical: a stub with no working code has been measured scoring most of the available reward on a bundle built this way. Rewrite the check to run the thing and assert on the result: import the module and call it, execute the binary and compare output, feed an input that only works if the repair is real. Where the property genuinely cannot be observed by running (a licence header, a required file being present at all), keep the text check but weight it as the existence check it is, never as evidence the task was completed. A positive test that pays for source tokens is a fail. Say what a stub would score against it.
    44	- TS-13 One property per test. A test asserting two unrelated properties hides which one failed and skews the weight; flag it.
    45	- TS-14 Check names state the property: `<property>` for positives, `negative_<cheat>` for negatives, and the name and the `description` match what the body checks. The `negative_` prefix is used if and only if the registered weight is negative (the prefix-and-sign rule is script-owned; your part is whether the name and the description match what the body actually checks).
    46	- TS-15 Negative checks use inverted polarity: the check returns True when the cheat is present, so an honest agent makes it return False and escapes the penalty; the negative weight does the punishing. Verify each negative check actually implements this direction; a check that returns True for honest work punishes honest agents. Each negative check should carry an inline comment stating the inversion.
    47	- TS-16 Anti-cheat negatives must fire on the cheat and ONLY on the cheat. Ask for each: could an honest solution trip this? Could a cheater slip past it?
    48	- TS-17 Comparisons are exact where the instruction promises exactness: byte equality, int equality, strict JSON equality. Watch the Python trap where `True == 1`: a bool/int confusion that lets `1` pass where `true` is required contradicts an exact-comparison promise - fail if the instruction implies strict types.
    49	- TS-18 Tolerances, when legitimate (floats), must be stated in the instruction. A hidden tolerance, or a missing one that makes honest float output fail, is a fail.
    50	- TS-19 Every graded behaviour must be learnable by the agent, and every promise the instruction makes must be graded by something. Learnable means stated in the instruction OR determinate in the input the agent is handed, meaning any directory `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image; the prompt may delegate the detail rather than restate it and need not name the file, so read the shipped input before calling anything undocumented. A check grading a rule of engagement rather than a property of the deliverable is the exception: a check on what the agent left alone, or on which paths it touched, needs a sentence that states the rule, because discovery establishes facts and never prohibitions (gate 01 IN-15). On the coverage direction, do not demand that a test cover every promise: a promise may legitimately be graded by a judged criterion instead, which is common for things no assertion can decide. Flag a promise nothing grades at all, never one the judged channel handles. (The full cross-walk happens in Gate 05; flag obvious mismatches you see now, especially error-object shapes and extra undocumented error cases.)
    51	- TS-20 Weights map to importance semantically. The scale means something: 5 = a core outcome, the deliverable's primary behaviour; 3 = an important sub-goal or key edge case; 1 = a minor detail or existence check. Negatives: -5 = a red-line cheat; -3 = a serious secondary violation; -1 = rare, minor. Judge every weight against what the test actually grades: a core-deliverable test carrying weight 1, or an existence/formatting check carrying 5, is wrong. Then check the ORDERING: if the instruction makes clear that property A matters more than property B, but B's test outweighs A's, the reward contradicts the task's own priorities - that is a fail, not a style call. Mild imbalance within the right ordering is a minor. All-identical weights are a fail (script).
    52	- TS-25 Positive tests grade earned work, never an absence. Read every positive test's decisive assertion: if it passes because something bad is missing (an empty violations list, a banned import that is absent, output that "does not contain" a thing), it hands its points to any solver that simply did nothing wrong, and it inflates the positive total so every real test is worth proportionally less. Absence belongs on the negative side with inverted polarity: a `negative_<cheat>` check that returns True when the bad behaviour IS present, registered with a negative weight. A positive-weight check whose pass condition is an absence is a fail; keep the detection logic, move it to a negative check.
    53	- TS-26 No intra-test double payment, in ANY pairing. Pair every test against every other test - negative against positive, positive against positive, negative against negative - and name the observable each reads. Two tests reading the same observable with the same decisive logic pay (or punish) one behaviour twice. Negative-vs-positive example: a positive test asserting output is identical across repeated runs alongside a negative test firing when output differs across runs is one determinism observation paid twice. Positive-vs-positive example: two tests that both decode the same fixture and assert the same field value is one correctness observation earning two weights, inflating that behaviour's share of the positive total. What is NOT a duplicate: two tests reading genuinely different aspects of the same object (the file parses; the parsed values are correct; the encoder round-trips it) - those add distinct signal. Same observable, same logic = keep one side, or rewrite one to read a different observable. Fail.
