    65	## Phrasing rules (each criterion)
    66	
    67	- JD-1 `description` starts with "The response" when the annotations' evaluation_target is final_answer or user_facing_message; starts with "The agent" when trajectory or state_change.
    68	- JD-2 Positive grammar ONLY, for both positive and negative criteria. Describe the behaviour as happening. Banned tokens: not, without, fails to, cannot, instead of. A negative criterion describes the BAD behaviour occurring and carries a negative weight; it never says the agent "does not" do a thing (that would subtract weight from the agent that behaved).
    69	- JD-3 No vague qualifiers: approximately, around, about, roughly, mostly, typically, often, properly, correctly, clearly, adequately - and any same-kind word. Also no threshold-free quantifiers: many, several, some, few, repeatedly. A judge has no way to decide how many is "many", so two judges reach different verdicts on the same transcript; state a count instead ("at least five shards", "on two or more occasions"). The criterion must be decidable from evidence, not from vibes. (The script fails many/several/few/repeatedly; "some" it only warns about, because the word is sometimes threshold-free and sometimes harmless, so you judge that one in context.)
    70	- JD-4 No square brackets, no placeholders ({{...}}, <TAG>, TODO), no tag prefixes inside the criterion text, no em/en dashes.
    71	- JD-5 Each criterion names ONE observable behaviour a judge could point to in a trajectory or artifact. If a judge would need to check two separate things, split it. The telltale shape is unrelated observables joined by "and": "The agent reads the spec and writes tests and validates edge cases" leaves the judge stuck when two of the three happened, because there is no way to record a partial result. Clearly compound criteria are a fail; a borderline case (two facets of one act) is a minor.
    72	- JD-6 Self-contained: the judge sees the trajectory and artifacts, not your head. "The agent uses the standard approach" is undecidable; "The agent verifies its encoder by round-tripping at least one shipped example" is decidable.
    73	
    74	## Content rules
    75	
    76	- JD-7 TRUTH AGAINST THE REFERENCE SOLUTION. Every criterion must be satisfied (or, for negatives, avoided) by the shipped oracle in solution/. A criterion that contradicts the reference implementation - rewarding behaviour the correct solution does not exhibit, or penalizing behaviour it does - would make the judge punish a correct agent. Check EVERY criterion against solve.sh / solve.py line by line. (Known real case: a criterion rewarded "does NOT advance the cumulator" while the reference solution advances it. That is an automatic fail.)
    77	- JD-8 NO OVERLAP WITH TESTS. Discriminator: name the observable each side reads. Same observable = overlap = fail, regardless of wording. Outcome-vs-process on different observables is fine.
