   114	- JD-S10 Every `description` starts with the prefix its evaluation_target demands: "The response" when the judge looks at the final answer or a user-facing message, "The agent" when the judge looks at the trajectory or the resulting state (script J9, after J8 confirms the description exists). The prefix binds the criterion to the correct judging surface.
   115	- JD-S11 No description uses negative grammar, positive and negative criteria alike (script J10): behaviour is described as happening and the judge decides whether it happened. A negative criterion describes the bad behaviour occurring and carries a negative weight; it never describes good behaviour negated. Nor does any description lean on vague qualifiers of the approximately/properly/clearly kind, or threshold-free quantifiers of the many/several/few/repeatedly kind (script J11), both of which make the criterion undecidable - the scripted list is not exhaustive, and "some" is a J11 warn rather than a fail, so judge that word and any same-kind word yourself as minor findings. Description text is also clean of square brackets, placeholder or template markers, em or en dashes and embedded tag prefixes (script J12).
   116	- JD-S12 The file has at least 8 criteria (more than 35 is a warn), includes at least one positive and one negative weight, does not give every criterion the same weight (a fail: identical weights mean the author never decided which behaviours matter most, so every verdict moves the reward by the same amount), and keeps the sum of absolute negative weights at or below the sum of positive weights (script G3..G6). The importance/weight pairing holds too (script J13): by `|weight|`, a critically important criterion carries 3 or 5, an important one 1 or 3. Re-judge the weight distribution yourself against JD-13's semantic mapping.
   117	- JD-S13 `tests/reward.toml` names `judge` in its `weights`, and `weights.judge` equals 0.5 divided by this file's positive criterion total at full float precision (gate 03 TS-10 owns the file; the script recomputes the value). A criterion added, removed or reweighted without updating reward.toml leaves the judged channel off its -0.5..0.5 share.
