    54	- AL-13 Answer-in-reach: could the agent reconstruct expected outputs from data plus instruction without doing the work (weak redaction, derivable keys, answers encoded in filenames/sizes/comments)?
    55	- AL-14 Reward-file tampering: instruction never mentions reward paths (Gate 01); test.sh clears stale reward files, locks `/logs/verifier`, writes no reward itself and ends with `exec rewardkit` (Gate 03 TS-6); no check can raise on agent output and leave the run without a reward (TS-29); no agent subprocess outlives its check to rewrite `reward.json` (TS-24); and `tests/reward.toml` still matches both channels' positive totals (TS-10). Re-confirm the combination end to end.
    56	- AL-15 Offline contract consistency: every layer must agree that the task runs with no network. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so there is no per-task policy to look up - the comparison is between the layers and that fixed fact, not between the layers and a value. Nothing may assume network at solve time or grade time: an instruction that tells the agent to download, a Dockerfile that fetches at run time rather than build time, a solution that installs what it needs, or a test that reaches an endpoint are each a fail. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines are NOT part of this comparison: the judge needs egress to score the judged channel and the agent harness needs egress to reach its own model, neither of which is the task being online. An offline task with an online verifier and an online agent harness is the normal combination rather than a contradiction. Do not raise a finding merely because the bundle never mentions the network; silence is the expected state. If the work genuinely requires an outside endpoint, say so here as a design finding against the offline contract.
    57	- AL-24 Filename-keyed behaviour: could a "solution" skip the real work by keying on input filenames instead of content (a lookup table from names to memorized answers; rename the file and it breaks)? If the data layout makes this profitable, something in the tests must defeat it (e.g. eval inputs the agent never saw, content-based checks).
    58	- AL-25 Nondeterministic output: could an agent that guesses, races, or emits different output on each run luck into reward? Grading must be robust to this (enough independent inputs that guessing cannot win, or repeated-run identity where the instruction promises determinism).
    59	- AL-26 Metadata truthfulness: read instruction.md, decide for yourself what kind of work DOMINATES the task, then compare against the declared metadata. `[metadata] category` must name that dominant kind of work: a mismatch is a fail, and it is still a fail when the declared category appears only as a side aspect of the task (a Debugging task with a security flavour declared as "Security" fails). `[task] description` must be an honest summary of what the instruction asks: misleading = fail, sloppy but true = minor. `keywords` and `tags` must relate to the task's actual subject matter: completely unrelated entries are a fail, weak-but-related entries are a warn.
