FILE Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Final_checklist.md
     1	# Final Checklist - Input Bundle QC
     2	
     3	This folder is the complete quality-control process for one input bundle (one task directory). It checks ONLY the input bundle: the files a task author wrote. It never looks at model runs or output bundles. The single exception is step 10, the execution gate, which runs the harness with the utility agents and a hand-built stub (no model involved) to prove the bundle actually grades correctly.
     4	
     5	If you were not given a bundle path, STOP and ask the user for the absolute path to the task directory before doing anything else.
     6	
     7	## What a bundle looks like
     8	
     9	```
    10	<task-name>/
    11	├── instruction.md               # prompt shown to the agent
    12	├── task.toml                    # config manifest
    13	├── environment/
    14	│   ├── Dockerfile               # agent-facing container
    15	│   └── <input-dir>/ ...         # ALL input the agent works on: one or more directories,
    16	│                                #   any names (data/, repo/, fixtures/...), each COPYed in
    17	├── solution/
    18	│   ├── solve.sh                 # oracle entrypoint (must score 1.0)
    19	│   ├── solve.py                 # optional
    20	│   └── TRUTH.md                 # author-side worked answer, never seen by the agent
    21	└── tests/                       # the reward function
    22	    ├── Dockerfile               # verifier image, built with tests/ as context, installs rewardkit
    23	    ├── test.sh                  # hardens the verifier, then runs rewardkit; writes no reward itself
    24	    ├── test_output.py           # deterministic rewardkit criteria, each with its weight
    25	    ├── judge.toml               # LLM-judged criteria (as important as the tests)
    26	    └── reward.toml              # combines both channels into the one `reward`
    27	```
    28	
    29	These files must exist (solve.py excepted), and nothing else may exist: the bundle root, `environment/`, `solution/` and `tests/` each hold exactly the entries shown (structure_qc S3). All input the agent works on lives in one or more directories next to `environment/Dockerfile`, named for what they hold (`data/` is the convention, `repo/`, `fixtures/`, `logs/` and so on are equally correct, and a task may ship several); `environment/` holds the Dockerfile and those directories and no loose files, every input directory holds at least one file, and every one is a COPY/ADD source in `environment/Dockerfile`, so it lands in the image where that COPY says, conventionally `/app/<dir>` (structure_qc S4). `reward.txt`, `reward.json`, `reward-details.json`, `ctrf.json`, `__pycache__/`, `.git/`, `.pytest_cache/`, `.DS_Store` are always forbidden. rewardkit imports every `.py` file and reads every `.toml` file at the top of `tests/`, which is one more reason `tests/` holds only the five files above.
    30	
    31	Three names are forbidden outright (structure_qc S3). A `cheat/` directory at the bundle root is a fail: a shipped bundle must not carry an attack on its own grader, and verifier hardening is reviewed by reading (gate 03 TS-24, TS-28). A `tests/test_weights.json` is a fail: test weights live on the `criteria.<name>(weight=W)` registrations in `test_output.py`, and a second copy drifts. A `tests/rubric.json` is a fail: the judged channel is `tests/judge.toml` only, and a rubric.json gives it a second source of truth. `tests/judge.toml` or `tests/reward.toml` missing is a fail (structure_qc S2).
    32	
    33	## Key harness facts every reviewer must know
    34	
    35	1. Harbor builds TWO containers. The agent works in a container built from `environment/Dockerfile` (each input directory lands where its COPY puts it, e.g. `/app/data` because of `COPY data /app/data`, `/app/repo` because of `COPY repo /app/repo`). Grading happens in a SEPARATE fresh container built from `tests/Dockerfile` with `tests/` as the build context. No input directory exists in the grading container. Any grade-time read of an input path (`/app/data` or any other COPY destination) is a guaranteed crash. `[verifier] environment_mode = "separate"` is REQUIRED in task.toml. In Harbor 0.24.0 a `[verifier.environment]` table is what makes the verifier run in its own container, and combining it with `environment_mode = "shared"` is an error; `"separate"` is still pinned so the intent is explicit in every bundle. A verifier that shares the agent's container never builds tests/Dockerfile, the agent-visible `/app/data` becomes the grading corpus, and agent-visible or agent-edited files grade the agent. Absent or any value other than `"separate"` is a fail (structure_qc T2).
    36	2. Artifacts MUST be declared as absolute container paths (e.g. `/app/decode.py`). Harbor copies each declared artifact out of the agent container and back into the verifier container using the declared path string verbatim (`artifact_handler.py: _upload_target_source` returns `artifact.source` verbatim; the copy is a `docker compose cp` on that raw string) and auto-creates parent directories (`ensure_dirs`). A relative or bare filename resolves against the image WORKDIR, which differs across images and providers (the template verifier image sets no WORKDIR, so a bare `decode.py` would land at `/decode.py` while the tests read `/app/decode.py`). Absolute paths are the only deterministic form; a non-absolute artifact entry is a fail (structure_qc T4). Tests must read each artifact at its exact declared absolute path.
    37	3. In separate mode `tests/` is NOT uploaded into the verifier at runtime when `tests/Dockerfile` exists (`verifier.py: skip_tests_upload`); the image must own it, so `tests/Dockerfile` must `COPY . /tests/`, install the pinned `harbor-rewardkit==0.2.1` (the version Harbor 0.24.0's own `rewardkit/pyproject.toml` names), pin `litellm==1.105.0`, and install every dependency the checks import. rewardkit declares `litellm>=1.83` with no upper bound, so an unpinned verifier image installs whatever PyPI serves at build time; a withdrawn litellm 1.107.0 stable shipped a `.pth` and a `sitecustomize.py` that fetched and executed remote code and reached the cloud metadata endpoint, inside the one container that holds the judge key and the hidden tests. Both pins are required (tests_qc.py K5, K8).
    38	4. Reward. Harbor 0.24.0 reads `/logs/verifier/reward.json` first and falls back to `/logs/verifier/reward.txt` only when no reward.json exists. reward.json must be a flat object of name -> number pairs; Harbor rejects NaN, infinity and text values. `tests/test.sh` hardens the verifier and then runs rewardkit over `/tests`; rewardkit writes reward.json (plus `reward-details.json` beside it with every criterion's result), and test.sh writes no reward bytes. Each criterion-bearing file at the top of `tests/` is one scoring input named by its file stem: `test_output` (the deterministic checks in `test_output.py`) and `judge` (the LLM-judged criteria in `judge.toml`). `tests/reward.toml` combines the two into the single `reward` value, each channel counting for half. rewardkit rounds the final value to 4 decimals. Wherever a check speaks of grading, a judged criterion is a graded check exactly as a test is.
    39	5. Network policy is per role (`[environment]`, `[verifier]`, `[verifier.environment]`, `[agent]`). Harbor's default is `"public"`, so an omitted key ships an online task. Every role uses `network_mode`; `allow_internet` must not appear.
    40	6. `[agent] user = "1000:1000"` runs the agent, and the oracle's `solve.sh`, as uid 1000, not root. Every directory a declared artifact is written into must be writable by that uid in the image `environment/Dockerfile` builds (for example `RUN mkdir -p /app && chown 1000:1000 /app` before `COPY data /app/data`), or the oracle dies with `PermissionError` before writing anything and Step 10 reads 0.000. A root-owned output directory is a fail (FC-S22, DS-28).
    41	
    42	## Authoritative task.toml template
    43	
    44	This is the exact shape every bundle's task.toml must match. Table order and key order within each table are mandatory (structure_qc T2). Extra keys are a fail.
    45	
    46	`schema_version` is exactly `"1.4"`, with per-role `network_mode`; any other schema value is a fail, and `allow_internet` must not appear anywhere. `docker_image` anywhere is a fail: it is a publish-time output, and an authored bundle pinning one pins an image that does not exist yet.
    47	
    48	```toml
