FILE Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/Starter.md
     1	# QC Starter - drop this file into a chat to run the full input-bundle QC
     2	
     3	You are the QC orchestrator for one input bundle. This file was dropped into the chat to start the complete QC process. Follow it exactly, from top to bottom, skipping nothing. You report defects; you never fix the bundle unless the user separately asks for fixes after seeing the report.
     4	
     5	## Non-negotiable working discipline (read this before anything else)
     6	
     7	This QC exists because defective bundles that passed a careless review reached production and cost real reward to honest agents. You are the last line before that happens again. The following rules bind every step below:
     8	
     9	- EVERY check runs. There are no optional checks, no "probably fine" checks, no checks skipped because the bundle looks clean, comes from a trusted author, or resembles a bundle that passed before. If a gate lists 25 checks, your report accounts for 25 checks.
    10	- READ, do not search. Searching for a keyword and concluding from the hits is not reviewing. You read every in-scope file from the first line to the last: every judged criterion word by word, every test function body, the whole instruction, the whole solution. grep is for LOCATING a line you will then read in context; it is never the basis of a verdict. "I searched for 'without' and found nothing" is not the same as "no criterion uses negative grammar", and a check closed on a search alone counts as a check not done.
    11	- A check is done only when you can quote the evidence. For a pass, you saw the actual lines that satisfy it. For a fail, you quote the offending line and re-verified it a second time. If you can point to nothing, the honest answer is "could not verify", never a silent tick.
    12	- Judgment checks get real judgment. Checks like overlap, double payment, trigger depth, and truth-against-the-oracle cannot be pattern-matched; they require you to hold two files in your head and reason. Spend the time. One criterion compared against one test at a time, every pair.
    13	- No batching shortcuts. Do not close "JD-1 through JD-14 verified" in one glance. Each ID gets its own pass over the material.
    14	- Slow is correct. A full QC of one bundle is expected to take a long session and read every byte of the bundle. If your pass felt quick, it was wrong; go back.
    15	
    16	## Step 0: gather your inputs
    17	
    18	You need two absolute paths before anything else:
    19	
    20	1. The QC folder (the directory this file lives in). It must contain: `Final_checklist.md`, `01_Instruction_QC.md`, `02_Data_Solution_QC.md`, `03_Tests_QC.md`, `04_Judge_QC.md`, `05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md`, `structure_qc.py`, `instruction_qc.py`, `tests_qc.py`, `judge_qc.py`, `exe_copy.py`.
    21	2. The bundle path (the task directory under review).
    22	
    23	If the user did not provide the bundle path, ask for it and stop until you have it. Never guess a path, never pick a bundle yourself, never hardcode one.
    24	
    25	Then read `Final_checklist.md`. It is the authority on the bundle contract, the harness facts, the authoritative task.toml and judge.toml templates, the authoritative test channel templates (test_output.py, reward.toml, test.sh, tests/Dockerfile), the verdict model, the output format, and the run order. Everything below assumes you have it loaded. Its Step 10 and Step 11 sections are long and are not needed until you reach those steps; read them there.
    26	
    27	## Step 1-4: run the four scripts
    28	
    29	Run each script against the bundle and capture the complete output:
    30	
    31	```
    32	python3 <QC_FOLDER>/structure_qc.py <BUNDLE_PATH>
    33	python3 <QC_FOLDER>/instruction_qc.py <BUNDLE_PATH>
    34	python3 <QC_FOLDER>/tests_qc.py <BUNDLE_PATH>
    35	python3 <QC_FOLDER>/judge_qc.py <BUNDLE_PATH>
    36	```
    37	
    38	Record every pass, warn and fail line plus the exit code (0 pass, 1 fail, 2 usage error). Do not stop on failures: the model gates still run so the report is complete in one round. Re-verify every script FAIL yourself against the actual file before repeating it in the report; scripts have edge cases.
    39	
    40	## Step 5-9: run the five model gates
    41	
    42	Run the gates in this order, one at a time, each as its own full pass:
    43	
    44	Each gate names one or two files it judges, and several more it reads as evidence for its cross-checks. Both columns are in scope: the "also reads" files are not optional, because the gate's highest-severity checks depend on them. Findings still belong to the gate's own subject; a defect you notice inside an evidence file is reported by whichever gate owns that file.
    45	
    46	| Gate | File | Judges | Also reads (evidence) |
    47	|------|------|--------|-----------------------|
    48	| 5 | `01_Instruction_QC.md` | instruction.md | task.toml, environment/Dockerfile, environment/, tests/test_output.py, tests/judge.toml |
    49	| 6 | `02_Data_Solution_QC.md` | environment/Dockerfile, environment/, solution/ (solve.sh, solve.py, TRUTH.md) | instruction.md, task.toml, tests/test_output.py, tests/judge.toml |
    50	| 7 | `03_Tests_QC.md` | tests/Dockerfile, test.sh, test_output.py (checks and weight registrations), reward.toml | instruction.md, the input directories environment/Dockerfile copies into the agent image, task.toml, tests/judge.toml (positive total for reward.toml) |
    51	| 8 | `04_Judge_QC.md` | tests/judge.toml | solution/, tests/test_output.py, tests/reward.toml, instruction.md, the input directories environment/Dockerfile copies into the agent image |
    52	| 9 | `05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md` | the whole bundle, cross-layer, including the client feedback checks CL-1..CL-10 | everything |
    53	
    54	Gate 9 carries one block that is not like the others: the client feedback checks CL-1..CL-10. Every one of them exists because the client found that exact defect in tasks we already delivered and measured what it cost, so none of them may be marked ✅ on a reading alone. Each names a number to compute or a file and line to quote, and the sign-off wants that number or that quote. The full wording is in `Final_checklist.md`; gate 05 repeats it so the gate stands on its own.
    55	
    56	Run the gates sequentially yourself, one at a time, and base every claim only on what you have actually read in that gate's own material, never on a conclusion carried over from an earlier gate.
    57	
    58	One ordering rule overrides "read everything first": in gate 6, leave `solution/TRUTH.md` until last. It is the author's worked answer, naming the intended defect, the traps and the solution, so reading it early tells you what to see and you will see it, and an unsolvable task will read as solvable because the answer was handed to you. Decide the solvability check on the agent-visible material alone, write down your conclusion, then open TRUTH.md to check it. Verify with it, never orient by it.
    59	
    60	For every gate, without exception:
    61	
    62	- Work from the full contents of every bundle file in that gate's scope.
    63	- Work through the gate's checks one by one. A gate's "Reference: what the script enforces" section is not among them: the script owns those, so read them for context, raise no finding from them, and only confirm the failures the script actually reports.
    64	- Copy the gate's checklist verbatim into the report, mark each item ✅ verified or ❌ problem found or could not verify.
    65	- Ground every claim in a line you actually read, and quote it.
    66	- Re-verify every FAIL a second time before reporting it.
    67	- If you cannot verify something, report "could not verify"; never guess and never assert from memory of how similar bundles usually look.
    68	- Thoroughness beats speed. A missed defect costs far more than a slow pass.
    69	
    70	## Step 10: execution gate
    71	
    72	Read and follow the `Step 10` section of `Final_checklist.md` exactly, together with the `⏸️ NOT RUN` row in its `Verdicts` section and the Step 10 lines of its consolidated sign-off. That section carries the preflight, the harbor version this bundle shape assumes, the tests-only copies built by `exe_copy.py`, the harbor commands, the score every run must read, where to read it from, and what to report when the machine cannot run the gate at all. Nothing about this step is decided here.
    73	
    74	## Step 11: final coverage and overlap re-check
    75	
    76	Read and follow the `Step 11` section of `Final_checklist.md` exactly. It names the files the re-check uses, how to map coverage in both directions, how judged criteria divide, and the two sections the report needs. Sign-off needs this report clean.
    77	
    78	## Final report
    79	
    80	End with one consolidated report in the fixed output format from `Final_checklist.md`, human-readable from the first line:
    81	
    82	1. The verdict line at the very top: `Verdict: ❌ FAIL (n fails, n minors, n warns)` (or ⚠️ WARN / 🟡 MINOR / ✅ PASS). The worst finding anywhere decides: fail = must be fixed and re-run; minor = fix or get tasker approval; warn = push-ready once the tasker approves; pass = clean.
    83	2. The per-gate summary table: one row per script, one per model gate, one for Step 10, one for Step 11, each with its own ❌/🟡/⚠️/✅ verdict and counts, and the overall verdict as the last row. Step 10 is the only row that may read `⏸️ not run` with zero counts; that state never changes the overall verdict, so say under the table that the bundle is not cleared to ship until it is run.
    84	3. Findings, grouped by severity, worst first: ❌/🟡/⚠️ + gate and check ID + what is wrong in one sentence + file and line + quoted evidence + Bug/Error or Suggestion/Improvement.
    85	4. The consolidated sign-off checklist from `Final_checklist.md`, every item marked ✅ or ❌; every ❌ must have a matching entry in Findings.
    86	
    87	No conversational filler, no offers of further help, no fixes. The report is the deliverable.

