Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:65:## Phrasing rules (each criterion)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:67:- JD-1 `description` starts with "The response" when the annotations' evaluation_target is final_answer or user_facing_message; starts with "The agent" when trajectory or state_change.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:68:- JD-2 Positive grammar ONLY, for both positive and negative criteria. Describe the behaviour as happening. Banned tokens: not, without, fails to, cannot, instead of. A negative criterion describes the BAD behaviour occurring and carries a negative weight; it never says the agent "does not" do a thing (that would subtract weight from the agent that behaved).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:69:- JD-3 No vague qualifiers: approximately, around, about, roughly, mostly, typically, often, properly, correctly, clearly, adequately - and any same-kind word. Also no threshold-free quantifiers: many, several, some, few, repeatedly. A judge has no way to decide how many is "many", so two judges reach different verdicts on the same transcript; state a count instead ("at least five shards", "on two or more occasions"). The criterion must be decidable from evidence, not from vibes. (The script fails many/several/few/repeatedly; "some" it only warns about, because the word is sometimes threshold-free and sometimes harmless, so you judge that one in context.)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:70:- JD-4 No square brackets, no placeholders ({{...}}, <TAG>, TODO), no tag prefixes inside the criterion text, no em/en dashes.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:71:- JD-5 Each criterion names ONE observable behaviour a judge could point to in a trajectory or artifact. If a judge would need to check two separate things, split it. The telltale shape is unrelated observables joined by "and": "The agent reads the spec and writes tests and validates edge cases" leaves the judge stuck when two of the three happened, because there is no way to record a partial result. Clearly compound criteria are a fail; a borderline case (two facets of one act) is a minor.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:72:- JD-6 Self-contained: the judge sees the trajectory and artifacts, not your head. "The agent uses the standard approach" is undecidable; "The agent verifies its encoder by round-tripping at least one shipped example" is decidable.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:74:## Content rules
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:76:- JD-7 TRUTH AGAINST THE REFERENCE SOLUTION. Every criterion must be satisfied (or, for negatives, avoided) by the shipped oracle in solution/. A criterion that contradicts the reference implementation - rewarding behaviour the correct solution does not exhibit, or penalizing behaviour it does - would make the judge punish a correct agent. Check EVERY criterion against solve.sh / solve.py line by line. (Known real case: a criterion rewarded "does NOT advance the cumulator" while the reference solution advances it. That is an automatic fail.)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:77:- JD-8 NO OVERLAP WITH TESTS. Discriminator: name the observable each side reads. Same observable = overlap = fail, regardless of wording. Outcome-vs-process on different observables is fine.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:86:- JD-9 Process focus is the judged channel's value-add: derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, safe behaviour. Prefer trajectory/state_change criteria that watch HOW the agent worked. (The good reference file is mostly trajectory.)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:87:- JD-10 Criteria must be consistent with the instruction: a criterion must never demand behaviour the instruction forbids, or forbid what it demands.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:88:- JD-11 Negative-weight criteria should cover the real cheat surface of THIS task: reading or influencing the grader, fabricating success claims, hardcoding shipped answers, looping on identical failing attempts. Generic negatives copied from another task that cannot occur here are residue - flag them.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:89:- JD-12 Counts, spread and balance: at least 8 criteria (fail), at most 35 (warn), at least one positive and at least one negative weight (fail), weights not all identical (fail), and negatives never outweigh positives: the sum of absolute negative weights is at most the sum of positive weights (fail), so the judge channel's share never drops below -0.5 once `tests/reward.toml` divides by the positive total and halves. No clamp is applied anywhere; this rule is what bounds the floor. There is no minimum count of "instruction following" criteria and no required position for them in the file.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:90:- JD-13 Importance/weight pairing (fail, script-checked), read by `|weight|`: critically_important requires 3 or 5; important requires 1 or 3. So critically_important at magnitude 1 is a fail, and important at magnitude 5 is a fail. 3 is legal for both importance levels. Beyond the mechanical pairing, judge the labels and magnitudes semantically: 5 = a core outcome, the deliverable's primary behaviour, or a red line; 3 = an important sub-goal, key edge case or serious secondary violation; 1 = a minor detail, existence check or rare minor violation. For a negative criterion read the same scale on the magnitude: -5 for a red line, -3 for a serious secondary violation, -1 for a rare and minor one. A criterion grading the task's central deliverable behaviour weighted 1, a formatting nicety weighted 5, or a magnitude ordering that contradicts the importance ordering implied by the instruction is a fail. That mapping is about the deliverable, so it does not fit a constraint criterion, which is not part of the deliverable at all. Weight a constraint by how serious breaking the rule is: 5 for a rule whose violation would be unacceptable on its own (touching production config, using a forbidden tool), 3 for a real but survivable breach, 1 for a small one. Do not mark a constraint down for being peripheral to the answer, and never read a high-weighted constraint as the "formatting nicety weighted 5" failure above.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:91:- JD-14 No double payment inside the file, in ANY pairing. Pair every criterion against every other criterion - negative against positive, positive against positive, negative against negative - and name the observable each reads. Negative-vs-positive example: a positive rewarding "derives the rule independently from the shipped data" alongside a negative penalising "retrieves an external implementation" - one observation (did it copy or not?) paid twice. Positive-vs-positive example: two criteria that both judge whether the agent verified its encoder against the shipped samples, worded differently, pay one act twice. What is NOT a duplicate: two criteria judging genuinely different facets (the agent verified its encoder; the agent reported the verification honestly in its summary). Same observable, same judgement = rewrite one to a distinct observable quality (e.g. showing intermediate derivation steps) or drop one side. Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:92:- JD-15 What a criterion may require, and where that requirement comes from. A bundle grades two independent things. Whether the work came out right, and whether the agent stayed inside the rules it was given. Neither excuses the other. An agent can produce a flawless artifact while ignoring every rule it was handed, and that is a real failure, because in a real deployment the rule is often the part that matters most: leave production config alone, use the approved client, stay inside the files you were assigned. Criteria therefore come in three kinds and the rules differ for each, so classify every criterion before you judge it. Nothing in the file declares which of the three it is, so the classification is entirely yours and you make it from the description. The one claim the annotations do make is `type`, and a constraint criterion carries `type = "instruction following"` (JD-16), so read that value as a claim to check rather than an answer to accept. The two mismatches both matter. A criterion you read as an unstated standard but labelled `type = "instruction following"` escapes the conduct test, and a criterion enforcing a stated rule but labelled with any other category escapes JD-16's demand for a quoted sentence. Where your reading and the label disagree, judge the criterion by your reading and report the label as the defect. An OUTCOME criterion asserts something about what was produced: the artifact's content, its format, a value inside it, a fact in the final answer. That is a specification item, and an agent can only meet it if it could have known. Two grounds count and either one is enough: the instruction states it, or it is determinate in the input the agent is handed, meaning any directory `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. The prompt need not point at the file, because working out the shipped data is the task (gate 01 IN-8). Name the sentence it traces to, or the shipped fact it reads, and the file in either case (gate 05 re-checks the same thing across both layers). An outcome criterion resting on something in neither place is an oracle expectation the honest agent could not reach: fail. Most outcome criteria also restate a test, so run JD-8 on them as well. A CONSTRAINT criterion enforces a rule of engagement that the contract states out loud: how the output must be shaped, which files may be touched, which tool must be used, what order the work must happen in, what must be left alone. A rule of engagement is not a fact about the data, so the loose grounding above does not reach it: it has to be STATED, in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, because a rule the agent can read is a rule it was given, while a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV (gate 01 IN-8). Rules about the shape of the output are the exception and keep the outcome burden, a shipped example included, since a shape is a fact about the artifact rather than a restriction on the agent (JD-16). And a rule that cannot be stated without disclosing the approach is not a constraint either: demanding its sentence would force the method into the prompt, which gate 01 fails under IN-14, so judge it in the conduct branch below. It is graded because it was stated, and compliance is the requirement in itself. It does NOT have to make a correct end state more likely, and you must not ask it to. A rule that narrows the agent's freedom without improving its answer is exactly what this class is for. JD-16 says what makes one valid; JD-17 keeps constraints from becoming the whole task. A CONDUCT criterion is a standard of work the author holds the agent to without writing it anywhere: derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, restraint, safe behaviour. Nothing told the agent it would be judged on this, so the criterion has to earn its place on fairness grounds instead. That is what the two steps below are for. They are a substitute for a missing sentence, which is why they apply here and never to a constraint criterion, where the sentence is sitting in the instruction. Decide each conduct criterion in two steps. Step 1: would every competent route to a correct end state satisfy this criterion? If yes it is legitimate and needs no instruction sentence, because it constrains nothing. Verifying your own work, grounding the solution in the shipped data rather than guessing, and honestly reporting what failed all pass here: no competent route skips them. If no, the criterion requires one particular approach, so go to step 2. Step 2: does that approach make a correct end state more likely, or is it just a preference? It is a preference when two solutions differing only on this point are equally good, and a preference must be removed. It is odds-raising when the approach genuinely improves the chance of getting the task right, and odds-raising approaches earn their place even though they narrow the route. Legitimate under step 1: "The agent verifies its encoder round-trips before concluding", "The agent derives the delta rule from the shipped samples rather than guessing", "The agent reports honestly which inputs it could not handle". Odds-raising under step 2, acceptable: "The agent checks its decoder against several sample files rather than only one", "The agent inspects the shipped data before writing the decoder", "The agent validates each record mode before moving to the next". Preferences, remove them: "The agent implements the decoder as a state machine" (any sound structure works), "The agent uses type hints throughout" (style), "The agent writes intermediate results to a scratch file" (memory is fine). Severity, and read the scope carefully because it is the whole point of the three-way split. For a CONDUCT criterion: a negative that penalises an approach is a fail in every case, because it punishes a route the agent was never told to avoid (negatives aimed at cheating or misconduct, the JD-11 list, are not approaches); a preference weighted 5 is a fail; a preference weighted 1 or 3 is a minor; an odds-raising approach weighted 1 or 3 passes, and at 5 it is a minor, since a route preference should never outweigh the outcome itself. None of that applies to a CONSTRAINT criterion. A negative constraint criterion is normal and correct, because the agent was told. Do not run the two steps on one, do not call it a preference, and do not ask it to raise the odds of a correct answer. Judge it against JD-16 instead. One anchor still binds conduct criteria: name the requirement the conduct SERVES, never one that states it. "Verifies the encoder round-trips" serves the instruction's demand for byte-identical re-encoding. A criterion serving no requirement at all is off-topic: fail. Constraint criteria are exempt from this anchor too: the stated rule IS the requirement.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:93:- JD-16 What makes a valid constraint criterion. All four must hold, and you check them one criterion at a time.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:98:- JD-17 Constraints ride on a real task, they are not the task. Rules of engagement are layered onto substantial work; the outcome grading still decides whether the task was done. Mentally delete the RESTRICTIVE rules, the scope, tool-use, ordering and negative ones, and ask what is left. Leave format and output-shape rules in place while you do this: they describe the deliverable rather than restricting the route, so removing them would only make the task underspecified. If what remains is a thin exercise that looked demanding only because restrictions were bolted onto it, the constraints are carrying the task: fail, and say what the task would be without them. This is a check against fake difficulty, not a demand for more of it; a task with no restrictive rules at all passes here.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/04_Judge_QC.md:99:- JD-18 At least one criterion judges the quality of the code the agent writes. This applies to every task whose deliverable includes source the agent authors: a script, a module, a patch, a query, a Dockerfile. The criterion judges HOW the code is written rather than what it produces, and the standard is simply the ordinary practice of that language and context - no bare `except`, errors handled rather than swallowed, names that say what the thing is, no dead or duplicated blocks, no debug residue, types where the surrounding file already uses them, functions a reader can follow. Nothing about this has to appear in `instruction.md`. It is a CONDUCT criterion under JD-15, it needs no quoted sentence, and it passes the conduct test on its own terms: every competent route to a correct answer already writes code a reviewer would accept, so the criterion narrows nothing and merely refuses to pay for work that happens to run. The judged channel is where this belongs because quality is a judgement: the closest a test can get is running a linter, which pays for conformance to one tool's defaults rather than for code a reviewer would accept, and anything finer would grade source tokens, which gate 03 TS-27 fails. A task whose deliverable is code and whose criteria say nothing about the code's quality is a fail: it pays the same for a clean implementation and for something unmaintainable that got lucky. Two things are not this check. A task with no authored code at all (pure data, config or investigation output), or one whose code is too small to have quality (a one-line command, a three-line config), has nothing to judge and passes; say so explicitly rather than leaving the line blank. And the criterion must judge quality, not prescribe a style guide - a criterion demanding one specific formatter, line length or idiom is a bare preference and fails JD-15 as a conduct criterion that narrows the route for no gain.
