Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:25:- TS-1 Nothing under tests/ may unconditionally read an agent input path (`/app/data` or any other input COPY destination) at grade time. Guaranteed crash. Fail. Comments and docstrings that merely mention the path are fine (the script skips them), and a read behind an existence check or env override is safe (the guard falls through to a fallback when the path is absent) - not flagged.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:26:- TS-2 `tests/Dockerfile` must `COPY . /tests/` (the image must own the test files: in separate mode Harbor 0.24.0 does not upload tests/ when tests/Dockerfile exists) and install exactly `harbor-rewardkit==0.2.1`, the version Harbor 0.24.0 pins. An unpinned, ranged or different version is a fail. The same line must pin litellm with `==` (the reference pin is `litellm==1.105.0`, what the agent image pins): rewardkit accepts `litellm>=1.83` with no upper bound, so an unpinned verifier takes whatever PyPI serves at build time, and a withdrawn litellm 1.107.0 stable shipped site-packages hooks that fetched and ran remote code in the container holding the judge key. Unpinned or a quarantined version is a fail; another exact version is a warn needing tasker approval.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:27:- TS-3 `tests/Dockerfile` should `RUN mkdir -p /app` (reference-task convention). The harness auto-creates artifact parent dirs at upload time, so absence is a warn, not a crash.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:28:- TS-4 `tests/Dockerfile` must install every third-party package test_output.py imports other than rewardkit (mind pip aliases: yaml/pyyaml, PIL/pillow, sklearn/scikit-learn) and every tool the scripts invoke (`util-linux` for `setpriv` or `runuser`), on Python 3.12 or newer, which rewardkit requires. pytest is not required. The agent image's packages are NOT available here.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:29:- TS-5 Any ground truth the tests need (expected values, fixtures, generators) must live inside tests/ itself, never be read from agent-writable paths.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:33:- TS-6 rewardkit writes the reward; test.sh writes none. test.sh follows the template: it clears stale reward files (`reward.txt`, `reward.json`, `reward-details.json` under `/logs/verifier`), locks the log directory, restricts `/tests`, and its last line is `exec rewardkit /tests --workspace /app --output /logs/verifier/reward.json`. rewardkit then writes `reward.json` and `reward-details.json` beside it. test.sh writes no reward bytes itself (no `echo` or `printf` into a reward file, no Python that writes one), runs nothing after rewardkit, and has no branch that skips it. If rewardkit fails, no reward.json exists and Harbor records a verifier error; that is the intended outcome for an authoring defect, and Step 10 makes such a bundle unshippable. Bad agent output must never be what makes rewardkit fail: that is TS-29. Trusting a pre-existing reward file is a fail. The same verifier error is also the intended outcome of a real agent run with no `OPENAI_API_KEY` (rewardkit raises "Missing credentials") or with no `/logs/agent/trajectory.json` (rewardkit raises "Trajectory not found"): a run whose judged half was never scored has no reward to report, so neither is a finding and neither may be worked around in test.sh. Step 10 avoids both by running the tests-only copy `exe_copy.py` builds.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:34:- TS-7 Computes the reward exclusively from tests/ ground truth plus the uploaded artifacts. Never from anything the agent could have written outside the declared artifacts.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:35:- TS-8 No skip switches (`SKIP_TESTS=1`, `if [ -f /app/skip ]`, env-var bypasses).
