Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:37:- TS-10 `tests/reward.toml` combines the channels correctly. It holds exactly one `[[reward]]` table with `name = "reward"`, `aggregation = "weighted-sum"` and `weights` naming exactly `test_output` and `judge`, plus one `[scoring.test_output]` table with `aggregation = "weighted-sum"`, and no other keys or tables. `weights.test_output` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive weights registered in test_output.py, and `weights.judge` equals 0.5 divided by the sum of positive criterion weights in judge.toml, both written at full float precision (Python's `repr` of the division). Recompute both from the actual files. The final reward is then half of (test weight earned / test positive total) plus half of (judge weight earned / judge positive total): 1 when every positive passes and no negative fires, 0 for doing nothing, -1 at worst. No clamp exists anywhere and none is needed, because neither channel's negatives may outweigh its positives (TS-23, gate 04 JD-12). A weight left stale after a check or criterion changed is a fail: the score then stops running from -1 to 1. No other top-level `.py` file under tests/ defines criteria, no other `.toml` carries `[judge]` or `[[criterion]]`, and no subdirectory of tests/ holds either, since rewardkit would score each one as an extra input.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:41:- TS-11 Deterministic: the same submission must earn the same score every run. Step 10 runs the oracle five times and requires 1.000 each time, but that only catches drift that happens to show up in five tries. Reading catches the rest, so walk the tests hunting for each of these by name: wall-clock or date values in an assertion, including "today" and elapsed-time bounds; unseeded `random` or `uuid`; iteration over a `set`, or over a `dict` built from a `set`; `glob`, `os.listdir`, `os.walk` or `Path.iterdir` consumed without sorting; tests that depend on running in a particular order or that mutate state a later test reads; timing margins tight enough to lose on a slow machine; locale, timezone or default-encoding dependence; hash-order effects; any network call at all. Because reward here is weighted rather than pass/fail, a single flaky check worth 3 of 53 points does not flip the verdict, it just moves the number a little on every run. That is invisible in one run and it is exactly the noise that ruins a training signal, since the agent is scored for something it did not do. Name the specific line, not the general worry.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:42:- TS-12 Outcome-driven: tests grade the final state and artifact behaviour, never the agent's command history or console output. This holds for stated rules of engagement too, and it decides which of them a test may own. In separate mode the verifier sees only the declared artifacts, so a test can grade scope or another effect only when the affected paths are declared artifacts; otherwise the judged channel owns it. A scope test that passes because something is absent is a negative (TS-25). A tool-use or ordering rule is not test material at all: proving it needs the command history, so the judged channel owns it, where the judge reads the trajectory (gate 04 JD-16). A test reaching into command history to check one is a fail even though the rule itself is legitimate. Where a test can grade a stated rule it should, since an assertion returns the same verdict every run and a judge does not, so a format, output-shape or declared-artifact scope rule left entirely to the judged channel is a real finding. Gate 04 JD-16 raises it, because deciding it needs the judged criteria in front of you; note it here if you spot it and leave the verdict to that gate.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:43:- TS-27 Reading the agent's source is not proof the agent did the work. Any assertion whose subject is the text of a file the agent wrote, rather than what that file does when run, grades appearance instead of behaviour. The shapes to look for are `assert "token" in source`, a regex over source text, a check that some identifier or import is present, a line count, or a diff size. Names like `x_was_repaired_not_bypassed` are a strong hint, since a check that has to claim it is not being fooled usually is. These pay out for typing the right words. An agent that writes a stub doing nothing and mentions the expected identifiers in a comment passes every one of them, which is not a hypothetical: a stub with no working code has been measured scoring most of the available reward on a bundle built this way. Rewrite the check to run the thing and assert on the result: import the module and call it, execute the binary and compare output, feed an input that only works if the repair is real. Where the property genuinely cannot be observed by running (a licence header, a required file being present at all), keep the text check but weight it as the existence check it is, never as evidence the task was completed. A positive test that pays for source tokens is a fail. Say what a stub would score against it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:44:- TS-13 One property per test. A test asserting two unrelated properties hides which one failed and skews the weight; flag it.
