Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:49:- TS-18 Tolerances, when legitimate (floats), must be stated in the instruction. A hidden tolerance, or a missing one that makes honest float output fail, is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:50:- TS-19 Every graded behaviour must be learnable by the agent, and every promise the instruction makes must be graded by something. Learnable means stated in the instruction OR determinate in the input the agent is handed, meaning any directory `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image; the prompt may delegate the detail rather than restate it and need not name the file, so read the shipped input before calling anything undocumented. A check grading a rule of engagement rather than a property of the deliverable is the exception: a check on what the agent left alone, or on which paths it touched, needs a sentence that states the rule, because discovery establishes facts and never prohibitions (gate 01 IN-15). On the coverage direction, do not demand that a test cover every promise: a promise may legitimately be graded by a judged criterion instead, which is common for things no assertion can decide. Flag a promise nothing grades at all, never one the judged channel handles. (The full cross-walk happens in Gate 05; flag obvious mismatches you see now, especially error-object shapes and extra undocumented error cases.)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:51:- TS-20 Weights map to importance semantically. The scale means something: 5 = a core outcome, the deliverable's primary behaviour; 3 = an important sub-goal or key edge case; 1 = a minor detail or existence check. Negatives: -5 = a red-line cheat; -3 = a serious secondary violation; -1 = rare, minor. Judge every weight against what the test actually grades: a core-deliverable test carrying weight 1, or an existence/formatting check carrying 5, is wrong. Then check the ORDERING: if the instruction makes clear that property A matters more than property B, but B's test outweighs A's, the reward contradicts the task's own priorities - that is a fail, not a style call. Mild imbalance within the right ordering is a minor. All-identical weights are a fail (script).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:52:- TS-25 Positive tests grade earned work, never an absence. Read every positive test's decisive assertion: if it passes because something bad is missing (an empty violations list, a banned import that is absent, output that "does not contain" a thing), it hands its points to any solver that simply did nothing wrong, and it inflates the positive total so every real test is worth proportionally less. Absence belongs on the negative side with inverted polarity: a `negative_<cheat>` check that returns True when the bad behaviour IS present, registered with a negative weight. A positive-weight check whose pass condition is an absence is a fail; keep the detection logic, move it to a negative check.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:53:- TS-26 No intra-test double payment, in ANY pairing. Pair every test against every other test - negative against positive, positive against positive, negative against negative - and name the observable each reads. Two tests reading the same observable with the same decisive logic pay (or punish) one behaviour twice. Negative-vs-positive example: a positive test asserting output is identical across repeated runs alongside a negative test firing when output differs across runs is one determinism observation paid twice. Positive-vs-positive example: two tests that both decode the same fixture and assert the same field value is one correctness observation earning two weights, inflating that behaviour's share of the positive total. What is NOT a duplicate: two tests reading genuinely different aspects of the same object (the file parses; the parsed values are correct; the encoder round-trips it) - those add distinct signal. Same observable, same logic = keep one side, or rewrite one to read a different observable. Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:55:- TS-29 Checks never raise. Any exception inside a check aborts the whole rewardkit run, so no reward is written and an agent output that merely breaks one check turns into a verifier error instead of a score. Every read of agent output (open, parse, index, convert, subprocess) is guarded so bad, missing or hostile output makes the check return False; a negative check returns False unless the cheat is positively seen. Each check returns a bool, never a number, None or a string. Nothing at module level does work beyond defining constants, checks and registrations, because rewardkit imports the module. Trace each check against an empty workspace and against garbage in every artifact.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:57:## Weight registration rules (script-owned, here for context)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:59:- TS-30 Agent file modes are survivable. The oracle installs its program with `cp`, which leaves mode 0644; a real agent writes it through an editor that renames a temp file into place, so the same program arrives at 0600, owned by uid 1000. Harbor hands `/app` to the verifier with those modes intact, and the verifier runs as root, so reading the file is only a problem once a check drops privileges to run it (`setpriv --reuid=nobody`, `runuser`, or Popen `user=`). Then the unprivileged child cannot open the program, every check that executes it returns False on a perfectly correct submission, and the plain oracle run still reads 1.000, so nothing in QC notices until a real trajectory is graded and loses that whole tier. Any verifier that executes agent-written code under a dropped uid must make it readable first: chmod the program and the directories above it (root may), or run a copy the verifier staged itself. Relaxing or removing the privilege drop is not the fix; that trades this bug for TS-24. tests_qc H6 checks the three shapes statically and fails when it finds none; the proof is the `--owner-only` oracle run in Step 10, which reproduces the agent's 0600 modes and must still read exactly 1.000. A bundle that scores lower there is a fail. The owner's own execute bit survives that run (a file already executable becomes 0700, not 0600), so a check may require an executable artifact, but only when the instruction or an agent-visible spec says to make it executable; requiring a mode or a shebang nothing tells the agent about is a CL-5 hidden convention.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:60:- TS-21 Exact bijection between check functions and registrations. Every check is registered exactly once, at module level, with `criteria.<name>(weight=W)`. An unregistered check is scored by rewardkit at the default weight 1 without anyone choosing that; a registration naming no check of this file pulls in a rewardkit built-in or crashes the run; a check registered twice is scored twice. The weight lives on the registration and nowhere else: `tests/test_weights.json` is forbidden (structure_qc S3).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:61:- TS-22 W is an integer literal from {5, 3, 1, -1, -3, -5}. Zero, 2, floats (`5.0`), strings, a missing weight (rewardkit would default it to 1) and any other argument are all fails.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:62:- TS-23 Counts and balance: at least 10 checks (fail), at most 35 (warn); at least one positive and at least one negative check (fail); weights not all identical (fail); the sum of absolute negative weights is at most the sum of positive weights (fail), so the test channel's share of the reward never drops below -0.5.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:64:## Verifier execution isolation (only when tests execute agent code)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:68:- TS-24 If any check in test_output.py executes agent-produced code, verify all of the following; any miss is a fail:
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/03_Tests_QC.md:73:- TS-28 The verifier image is actually hardened, not just assumed to be. TS-24 asks you to reason about the attack; this asks you to confirm the defences exist. tests_qc H1 and H3-H5 check the mechanics and warns where it cannot tell whether a defence is needed; you decide whether it is needed here. The reference verifier does four things, and each one closes a specific hole: it installs `util-linux` so the checks can run agent code under `setpriv` as an unprivileged user; it makes `/tests` unreadable to that unprivileged user (`chmod -R go-rwx /tests`) so agent code cannot read the answer key it is being graded against; it removes any pre-existing reward file before grading begins, so an abort cannot leave a stale score standing as the result; and it locks the verifier log directory to root (`chown root:root` plus `chmod 755`) so nothing the agent spawned can write a reward into it. Use 755 rather than 700 there: root ownership is what blocks other writers, and Harbor reads the bind-mounted reward file back as the host user, so a directory it cannot traverse makes that read fail on Linux and errors the trial even though grading succeeded. A bundle that still uses 700 earns an H5 warn, not a fail. Decide by asking one question: does grading execute agent-produced code at all? If it does, all four are load-bearing and a missing one is a fail, so name the hole and the attack it opens. If grading only reads files the agent wrote and never executes them, privilege dropping is not required and the script's H3 warn about `/tests` permissions can be dismissed; say so explicitly rather than leaving it unaddressed. Clearing stale reward files and locking the log directory (H4, H5) matter either way. H1 is outside that judgement entirely, and so is K4. They fire only when grading calls a binary or imports a module the image never installs, which breaks grading whatever the threat model, so both are fails and the reasoning above never excuses them.
