Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:11:## Mindset
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:17:- DS-1 All input lives in one or more input directories under environment/ (any names: data/, repo/, fixtures/...), each holding at least one file and each copied into the image by environment/Dockerfile; environment/ holds only the Dockerfile and those directories. The names and the split are the task's choice: judge only that each directory is a coherent unit of input (a repository, a corpus, a set of rule files), not that it is called data/. Script-owned, here for context.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:18:- DS-2 Necessary: deleting the input directories must make the task unsolvable, and so must deleting any single one of them. If the agent could complete the task without ever opening an input directory, that directory is residue - fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:19:- DS-3 Referentially complete and truthful: every data file the instruction references exists here with the exact name; every claim the instruction makes about the data (counts, formats, fields, examples) is true of the actual bytes. Open the files and verify, never infer from file name only. Note this is a much weaker property than the data being enough to solve the task, which is DS-21. A bundle can pass DS-3 cleanly and still be impossible.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:20:- DS-4 NO ANSWERS IN DATA - strict, no exceptions. Nothing in any environment/ input directory may contain, encode, or make trivially derivable the expected final output of the task. A `*.solution.*` file, an answer key, a "labelled" file whose labels are the graded output, expected-output examples that the tests grade against - all fail. (Known real case: cache-adversary-synthesis shipped example_1.solution.json - that was a confirmed leak.) What is NOT a leak: a document describing the format, schema, protocol or rules the agent must work to. Contract material tells the agent what shape the answer takes; it does not hand over the answer, and a prompt is allowed to point at such a file instead of restating it (IN-8). So do not report one merely for existing. Read it before deciding, because the line is about content and not about the file being called a specification: a worked example carrying the graded values, a table of expected outputs, a walkthrough that computes a graded result, or prose stating the answer to a case anything grades is a leak whatever the document is titled. Confirm the answer genuinely is not recoverable by reading it, then move on.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:21:- DS-5 No template residue (read every TODO/FIXME/XXX/PLACEHOLDER hit in context yourself; whatever the script reports on those markers is a lead, not a verdict): a partial spec with "(redacted)" sections, generic sample_NNN/field_NNN corpora, or any file that reads as boilerplate from another task is a fail. The known-bad inherited pattern is a `<TAG>-v1.0-DRAFT.md` spec with redacted sections - its presence is an automatic fail. Data must be curated for this task per the task guidelines.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:22:- DS-6 Data files must make sense for the task story: formats parse, sizes are reasonable, damaged files are damaged on purpose (and the instruction says so).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:23:- DS-7 No em/en dashes in text data files (script-checked bundle-wide; re-verify any file the script skipped as binary if it is actually text).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:27:- DS-8 Must never COPY tests/ or solution/ content into the agent image, in any form.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:28:- DS-9 Must copy every input directory into the image at the path the instruction uses (conventionally /app/<dir>, e.g. /app/data, /app/repo), and the instruction must use those paths consistently.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:29:- DS-10 Solvability: every tool, interpreter, package or library that the instruction or the reference solution needs must be installed by this Dockerfile. Compare the instruction's demands and solve.sh/solve.py imports line by line against what the image installs. A named-but-missing tool is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:30:- DS-11 Offline contract respected. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so this is not a value to read and compare: assume no network and check that every layer can live with it. Everything the agent needs must be baked in at build time, and any runtime fetch is a fail - `pip install`, `apt-get`, `npm i`, `git clone` or `curl` reached for inside the task flow, in the instruction, or in the ORACLE's own path. Pay particular attention to the oracle: a `solve.sh` that installs a dependency it needs will simply fail at grade time, and a bundle whose own reference solution cannot run offline is broken rather than merely fragile. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines govern the judge and the agent harness reaching their models; they are not this check's business and do not license anything in the task flow. If the task genuinely needs an outside endpoint to be solvable, report that as a design finding against the offline contract.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:31:- DS-12 No secrets, no host paths, no `--privileged` assumptions, nothing that only works on the author's machine.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:32:- DS-28 Every artifact directory is writable by the agent user. `[agent] user = "1000:1000"` runs the agent, and the oracle's `solve.sh`, as uid 1000, not root. The directory each declared artifact is written into must be owned by uid 1000 (or world-writable) in the image this Dockerfile builds, for example `RUN mkdir -p /app && chown 1000:1000 /app` before `COPY data /app/data`. COPY and ADD write root-owned files unless they carry `--chown`, so a `chown` placed before a COPY into the same directory does not cover it. A root-owned output directory makes the oracle die with `PermissionError` before it writes anything, and Step 10 reads 0.000. Fail. structure_qc S8 replays the Dockerfile's `mkdir`, `chown`, `chmod`, `COPY --chown`, `WORKDIR`, `USER` and `useradd -u` lines and checks the mechanics; you confirm anything it cannot see, such as ownership set by a script the Dockerfile runs, a base image's own users, or an `ENTRYPOINT` that changes permissions at start.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:33:- DS-29 The openhands-sdk agent runtime is pre-baked. The harness runs the agent through the openhands-sdk wrapper from `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv`, and it only installs that runtime itself, over the network, when the venv is missing. With `[environment] network_mode = "no-network"` that install cannot run, so the agent never starts. Every `environment/Dockerfile` must therefore carry, in its final stage: the system packages `curl`, `ca-certificates` and `tmux`; a venv at exactly `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv`; `openhands-sdk==1.55.1 openhands-tools==1.55.1 litellm==1.105.0 fastapi` installed into it (those exact pins; fastapi unpinned; litellm 1.106.0 fails to import alongside the mcp package openhands pulls in); a build-time `/opt/openhands-sdk-venv/bin/python -c "import openhands.sdk"` so a broken install fails the build; and `chmod -R a+rX /opt/openhands-sdk-venv` so uid 1000 can run it. The recommended block, matching the harness's own installer:
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:50:- DS-30 The uid 1000 agent account exists. `[agent] user = "1000:1000"` runs the agent and the oracle as uid 1000, so the final stage of `environment/Dockerfile` must create that account with a home directory and point `HOME` at it; without them, tools that write under `$HOME` (uv, pip, the agent itself) fail. Put these right after `FROM`:
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:61:- DS-13 solve.sh exists and is the entrypoint. solve.py is optional; if absent, solve.sh alone must carry the full solution.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:62:- DS-14 The oracle must genuinely do the work: read the inputs, compute, and write every declared artifact to the exact paths the instruction promises. An oracle that copies a prebaked answer into place without computing it is a fail (it proves nothing about solvability and hides broken tasks).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:63:- DS-15 Walk through solve.sh mentally against the tests: would it plausibly score 1.0? Every artifact produced, formats matching what tests expect, edge cases the tests grade actually handled. If you can name a test it would fail, that is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:64:- DS-16 The oracle MAY read environment/ (allowed - it runs in the agent container where the input directories exist).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:65:- DS-17 The oracle must never read or reference tests/ (test_output.py, judge.toml, reward.toml) - an oracle that peeks at the grader is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:66:- DS-18 Idempotency: running solve.sh twice should not corrupt the result - warn only.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:67:- DS-19 Stack conformance: solution code must respect constraints the contract imposes on the agent (stdlib-only, specific language, which files may be touched, which tool to use, what to leave alone). A rule of engagement counts when it is stated in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, so open those files too: a rule may live there rather than in the prompt. If the contract binds the agent to constraints the oracle itself violates, flag it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:68:- DS-20 The oracle obeys the contract, independently of the tests. Passing every test (DS-15) is NOT proof: tests undercover, and an oracle can score 1.0 while violating a stated rule no fixture happens to trigger. Walk the oracle's logic against every rule the contract states, wherever that rule lives, including rules no test exercises. Ordered logic deserves special care: when the contract states a precedence or tie-break chain ("check A before B before C"), read the oracle's actual check order and compare it pairwise against the stated chain. The oracle is the ground truth of the task; when it disagrees with the contract, every agent that obeys the prompt gets punished for the oracle's bug. Any divergence between stated rule and oracle behaviour is a fail, even when no current test would catch it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:69:- DS-24 The oracle is an independent implementation, not a copy of the verifier. Open `solution/solve.py` and `tests/test_output.py` side by side and judge whether the oracle solves the task or merely re-runs the grader's own code. tests_qc measures shared substantive lines, fails at 35% and warns from 20%, but the number is only a prompt to look: a small shared block can be the whole answer, and a large one can be incidental. This matters because a copied oracle is guaranteed to score 1.000. It cannot disagree with the grader, so the Step 10 oracle run returns a green light while proving nothing about whether the task is solvable at all. Two independent implementations only agree when both are right, and that agreement is the only evidence the oracle run actually produces. Some sharing is legitimate. A verifier that must generate a corpus needs an encoder, and the oracle needs one too. What is not legitimate is the oracle importing, copying or restating the logic that decides the grade. Ask which part of the shared code is the thing being graded: if the answer is "the shared part", the oracle proves nothing and this is a fail. Keep overlap as low as the task allows even when it is under the limit, and say so when you see an author sitting just beneath the line.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:70:- DS-21 Solvable from what the agent actually receives. This is the mirror of DS-2: DS-2 asks whether the data is needed, this asks whether it is enough. Close `solution/` and `tests/` in your head and hold only what the agent holds, which is `instruction.md` plus everything under `environment/`. Nothing else reaches the agent. One question: could a determined expert, given unlimited time and skill and nothing but those files, actually arrive at the graded answer? If yes, this check passes, however brutal the task is. Difficulty is the point and it may come from anywhere. Traps, deliberately misleading surfaces, rules that only show themselves under one specific condition, data scattered across many files, a value in one file that only makes sense once another file is understood, chains where each step unlocks the next, edge cases that punish a naive reading, long grinding search. None of that is a defect and none of it belongs in your report. Working it out is the agent's job, and a task that gives up its answer easily is a worse task. The defect is different in kind: information the agent needs that exists in none of its files and follows from nothing in them. The clearest case is a value the agent must reproduce exactly while nothing it can see constrains or confirms it, such as a secret key, a private label, or an arbitrary constant taken from the author's head. There the agent has nothing to reason from and no way to tell a right guess from a wrong one, so skill stops mattering and the task is impossible rather than hard. Default to pass. To fail this check you must do two things: name the exact piece of information the solver cannot obtain, and show that it appears in no agent-visible file and follows from nothing in them. If you cannot do both, the task is hard, not impossible, and it passes.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:71:- DS-25 The exact output shape is reachable. DS-21 asks whether the agent can work out the answer; this asks whether it can work out what the answer has to look like. They fail separately, and this one fails silently, because an agent can compute every value correctly and still score zero for emitting them under names it had to invent. Do it mechanically. List every literal the grading side requires of the agent's output: JSON key names, section headings, CSV column names, error strings, enum values, filenames the tests open, ordering the tests assume. Read them out of `tests/test_output.py` directly rather than trusting a summary, and include the ones built at runtime rather than written as constants, since string formatting hides them from any search. Then for each one, find where the agent could learn it: the instruction, a specification or schema file under `environment/`, or the shipped data itself, including the interiors of any archive the agent is given. Name the file for each. Any required literal that appears in no agent-visible file is a fail, because no amount of skill produces a name nobody supplied. Report the count and the specific names, for example "18 of 24 required keys appear in /app/data/SCHEMA.md, these 6 appear nowhere". A shape the agent must invent and the grader then insists on is the single most common way a solvable task becomes unpassable.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:72:- DS-26 Nothing that gives away the answer is reachable anywhere in the agent image. DS-4 covers `environment/`, which is where reviewers look. This covers everywhere else, because the agent has a whole filesystem and the instruction only points at part of it. Read `environment/Dockerfile` line by line and follow what each step leaves behind. Check for: a COPY or ADD that lands solution material outside /app/data, a RUN step that writes a worked answer, an expected output or a reference artifact into `/tmp`, `/root`, `/opt`, `/usr/local` or the image's working directory; a build stage that produces the answer and is then copied from; a package, wheel, git checkout or archive whose contents include the solution; a leftover script, log, cache or history file from building the broken state. Bind mounts and volumes count too. Fail on anything from which the answer can be recovered without doing the work, and say exactly where it lives and how the agent would reach it. The test is not whether the file looks like an answer; it is whether an agent that finds it can shortcut the task.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:74:## TRUTH.md rules
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:78:- DS-22 TRUTH.md describes the bundle that actually shipped. This is the one thing no script can check: a digest comparison proves `solve.py` was not edited after the fact, but nothing proves the prose still describes the code. Read the `Root cause` and `Solution shape` sections against `solution/solve.py` and confirm they describe the same mechanism, not an earlier draft of it. Read the `Verification` section against the weight registrations in `tests/test_output.py` and the criteria in `tests/judge.toml` and confirm the stated counts, weight totals and named negatives are the real ones. Read the `Defect` section against the task and confirm each item marked focal, drift or trap really is one: a "trap" nothing in the data can spring, or a "focal" item no test grades, is a claim the bundle does not honour. A TRUTH.md that describes a different task, a superseded solution, or grading that does not exist is worse than none, because reviewers after you will trust it. Fail, and name the section and the contradiction.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:79:- DS-23 TRUTH.md is invisible to the agent. Confirm `environment/Dockerfile` copies only what the agent needs and never reaches outside `environment/` (structure_qc S7 checks the mechanics; you check intent, including a copy that reaches it by a wildcard or a build stage). Note that `solution/` now carries the whole answer twice, the oracle and TRUTH.md, so any route into it is a double leak. Confirm `instruction.md` never mentions TRUTH.md, since naming it tells the agent an answer document exists and invites a hunt for it. Any route by which the agent could read this file is a fail: it is the complete answer, so one leaked copy makes the task free.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:80:- DS-27 TRUTH.md describes the task, not how the task was made. It is a client delivery file. Its job is to state what the task breaks, what the solver must work out and how grading answers that, so a reader who has never seen the bundle understands the task itself. What does not belong is any narrative of the production process: how the bundle was generated, an archetype or template name, a generator seed, tooling or model credits, a changelog of what the author tried first, "this was built by" or "I then added", review and approval history, internal taxonomy. None of it describes the task, and shipping it hands the client a record of our process in place of a description of the work. The `Provenance` block is not an exception. Identifiers and digests belong there, because that is bookkeeping binding the document to the shipped bytes. Authoring attribution does not, and neither does anything naming the tool that produced the bundle. Generated bundles carry an `authored_by` value emitted by the generator; it is stale template output, it says nothing true about who built this task, and it ships to the client. Treat it exactly as you would the same claim in prose: it comes out. Fail on process narration and on authoring or tooling attribution, naming the section and quoting the line.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:82:## Checklist (copy verbatim into your report)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/02_Data_Solution_QC.md:115:## Severity guide
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:7:## Foundational checks first
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:11:## Client feedback checks (mandatory, none of them skippable)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:15:CL-1..CL-10 are specified in full - each rule, its client case and the damage it caused - in Final_checklist.md under "Client feedback checks", and are listed as actionable items in this gate's checklist below. Work them from there: compute or quote the evidence each one names, and report every hit under its CL id.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:17:## The alignment cross-walk (core of this gate)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:21:- AL-1 Everything graded is promised. The rule differs by layer, because tests and judged criteria relate to the instruction differently. Do not apply one rule to both. TESTS, strict: for each test, point to where the agent could have learned this would matter. Two grounds count and either one is enough. The instruction states it, or the fact is there to be found in the input the agent is handed: any directory under `environment/` that `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, whatever that directory is called. The prompt does not have to point at the file, because working out the shape of the data you were given is the task; what the fact does have to be is determinate, something the agent can read off the shipped input rather than something the input merely fails to contradict. `tests/` and `solution/` are never grounds, since the agent never sees them. One carve-out, and it is the same one the criteria carry below: where a test grades a rule of engagement rather than a property of the deliverable, what the agent left alone or which paths it touched, discovery does not ground it, because a prohibition cannot be inferred from data. Such a test needs a sentence stating the rule, or the expectation has to be one every competent route would meet anyway, which makes it conduct and moves it to the judged channel (gate 01 IN-15 finds the same defect from the prompt side). What fails is a test asserting a property that is in neither place, which is an oracle expectation that costs the honest agent reward for doing the task correctly. Fail; name the test, the expectation, and the files you searched. JUDGED CRITERIA, split by kind (JD-15 covers this inside gate 04; redo it here). The bundle grades two independent things, whether the work came out right and whether the agent stayed inside the rules it was given, so criteria come in three kinds and you classify before you judge. An OUTCOME criterion, which asserts something about what was produced, is a specification item and carries the same burden as a test: point to the instruction sentence, or to the determinate fact in the copied input, or fail it. A CONSTRAINT criterion enforces a rule of engagement the contract states out loud: how the output must be shaped, which files may be touched, which tool must be used, what order the work happens in, what is left alone. A rule of engagement is not the same kind of thing as a fact about the data, and the loose grounding above does not reach it: it must be STATED, in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. No amount of discovery substitutes, because a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV. Two exceptions sit on either side of that. A rule about the shape of the output keeps the test burden above, a shipped example included, because a shape is a fact about the artifact rather than a restriction on the agent. And a rule nobody could state without giving away the approach is not a constraint either: demanding its sentence would force the method into the instruction, which gate 01 fails under IN-14, so judge it as conduct. Quote the sentence and name its file. It is graded because it was stated, so compliance is the requirement in itself and you must not ask it to improve the odds of a correct answer. A negative constraint criterion is normal and correct here. What fails is a constraint criterion with no sentence to quote anywhere the agent can read, because that is an unstated rule costing the honest agent reward (gate 01 IN-15 finds the same defect from the instruction side). A CONDUCT criterion, which judges how the agent worked (derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, restraint, safety), has no instruction sentence behind it and needs none, because demanding one would force the method into the instruction, which gate 01 fails under IN-14. The two steps below exist precisely because nobody told the agent, so they apply here and never to a constraint criterion. Step 1: would every competent route to a correct end state satisfy this criterion? If yes it is legitimate, because it constrains nothing. If no, step 2: does the approach it requires make a correct end state more likely, or is it just a preference? A preference, where two solutions differing only on this point are equally good, must be removed; an odds-raising approach earns its place. A negative CONDUCT criterion penalising a legitimate approach is always a fail (negatives aimed at cheating or misconduct, gate 04 JD-11, are not approaches). A preference weighted 5 is a fail and weighted 1 or 3 is a minor; an odds-raising approach weighted 5 is a minor. Then name the requirement the conduct SERVES, not one that states it. A conduct criterion serving no requirement at all is off-topic and fails. Quote the criterion and say which step it broke.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:33:- AL-2 Everything promised is graded. Enumerate from `instruction.md` alone. The unit here is what the PROMPT promises, not everything any shipped file happens to say, so a spec that documents more than this task asks for does not turn its spare detail into ungraded promises. Where the prompt delegates instead of restating ("your output must conform to `schema.json`"), follow the pointer and enumerate the delegated rules the delegation actually invokes, not the whole file: otherwise one sentence collapses a forty-field schema into a single row, and one test touching one field looks like full coverage. For each requirement so enumerated, point to the test or the judged criterion that checks it; either channel satisfies this. An ungraded promise invites solutions that skip it. Strict fail; name the requirement and state that no test or criterion covers it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:34:- AL-3 Exact-shape agreement: output formats, error-object shapes, key names, exit codes, file paths, precedence orders. The classic bug: instruction demands {"error": "x", "version": N} while the test asserts the object equals {"error": "x"}. Compare every literal shape the agent can see against every shape anything grades, byte by byte. "Can see" means the instruction and anything shipped in the input directories environment/Dockerfile copies into the agent image, whether or not the prompt names them; read those files as part of the contract, because for most bundles the exact shapes live there rather than in the prompt. "Anything grades" means the tests AND any judged criterion that names a shape, since a criterion demanding one format while the contract states another traps the agent exactly as a test would. Any mismatch where following the contract loses reward is an automatic fail, and a shape that contradicts between the instruction and a shipped spec file is the same fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:35:- AL-4 Hidden behaviours: a behaviour of the DELIVERABLE that the tests, an outcome judged criterion, or the solution handles while the contract forbids or omits it, where the contract is the instruction together with whatever the copied input establishes (AL-1) (extra error strings when the contract says "do not invent new error strings", undocumented edge cases carrying graded weight in either layer). Fail. Conduct criteria are NOT hidden behaviours and never belong in this finding: JD-15 exempts them from needing a sentence anywhere, so a criterion about verification habits or honest reporting being absent from the contract is correct by design, not a defect.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:36:- AL-5 The oracle passes its own tests: trace solve.sh output through every test. Any test the oracle would fail proves the bundle broken. Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:37:- AL-6 The judged criteria agree with the solution and the instruction (JD-7/JD-10 deep pass): re-verify the three most load-bearing criteria yourself.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:38:- AL-27 Trigger depth: a rule only counts as graded when the test's input actually constructs the situation where the rule matters. A test can be named after a rule, tick the AL-2 mapping on paper, and still never exercise it because no fixture creates the triggering case. The classic shape is an ordering or precedence rule ("check the magic before the version"): unless some graded input carries BOTH faults at once, the order never matters and a solver checking things backwards still scores full reward. For every ordering, precedence, or tie-break rule the bundle grades, whether the instruction states it or the copied input establishes it (AL-1), trace the fixtures: for each adjacent pair in the chain, find the graded input with both conditions present and the assertion that the earlier one wins. For every other rule, ask "which graded input would come out differently if the solver broke exactly this rule?" - no such input means the rule is ungraded regardless of what the test is called. Fail; name the rule and the missing fixture.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:39:- AL-28 No penalty stacking. For every negative check in test_output.py AND every negative-weight criterion in judge.toml, name the single concrete agent action that triggers it (hardcoded the shipped answers; edited a file outside the allowed scope; read tests/; emitted nondeterministic output...). Group the negatives by triggering action, ACROSS both layers. One mistake must cost at most one maximum-severity penalty. In the tests, sum the absolute weights in each group: a combined penalty above 5 is stacked, the agent punished twice or three times for one decision and the reward floor distorted for everyone. In `tests/judge.toml`, stacking is two negative-weight criteria pricing one act (sum of absolute weights above 5). Across layers it is one act subtracting in the tests and again in a negative-weight criterion. Example shape: the same hardcoding act firing a -5 check and a -3 check costs 8 for one decision, and the same act firing a -5 judged criterion subtracts a third time in the other channel. Fix: keep the strictest single penalty and retarget or drop the rest, or carve the triggers into genuinely non-overlapping conditions so no single action fires more than one. Fail; show the group, its members, and what each one costs.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:41:## Metadata truth
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:43:- AL-7 No unearned claims: every number or claim presented as measured (scores, solve rates, "oracle scores 1.0", timing ladders) must be plausible and marked for what it is. A "Measured scores" block that is template residue - identical wording/numbers to sibling bundles, or claims about runs that produce files not in this bundle - is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:44:- AL-8 Difficulty honesty: the time estimates must be consistent with the actual task content. A task whose full solution is derivable from shipped examples in minutes cannot claim "8 hours expert". Judge the work: what must an expert actually derive, how much of it is shielded from shortcut. If the claim is implausible, fail and explain. There is no `difficulty` key in task.toml.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:45:- AL-9 Solution discussion never leaks into agent-visible surfaces. TRUTH.md and task.toml prose are author-side and may discuss the solution freely; TRUTH.md exists precisely to spell it out. `instruction.md` and everything under `environment/` must not. So do not report TRUTH.md for containing the answer, that is its job; report any path by which the agent could reach it (gate 02 DS-23, structure_qc S7).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:47:## Anti-cheat audit (adversarial pass)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:51:- AL-10 Hardcode shipped answers: if data contains labelled examples, could an agent score above the honest floor by memorizing them? The negative tests must catch exactly this.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:52:- AL-11 Grade-time environment abuse: tests reading anything agent-writable (input-path reads such as /app/data crash - Gate 03; but also /tmp state, env vars, marker files an agent could plant).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:53:- AL-12 Grader influence: could the agent's artifacts shadow a tool, monkey-patch a module, or pre-write a file the verifier trusts? Remember artifacts land at their exact declared absolute paths in the verifier container - check that neither test.sh nor any check in test_output.py executes or imports anything from agent-written paths except deliberately running the declared artifacts as subprocesses.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:54:- AL-13 Answer-in-reach: could the agent reconstruct expected outputs from data plus instruction without doing the work (weak redaction, derivable keys, answers encoded in filenames/sizes/comments)?
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:55:- AL-14 Reward-file tampering: instruction never mentions reward paths (Gate 01); test.sh clears stale reward files, locks `/logs/verifier`, writes no reward itself and ends with `exec rewardkit` (Gate 03 TS-6); no check can raise on agent output and leave the run without a reward (TS-29); no agent subprocess outlives its check to rewrite `reward.json` (TS-24); and `tests/reward.toml` still matches both channels' positive totals (TS-10). Re-confirm the combination end to end.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:56:- AL-15 Offline contract consistency: every layer must agree that the task runs with no network. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so there is no per-task policy to look up - the comparison is between the layers and that fixed fact, not between the layers and a value. Nothing may assume network at solve time or grade time: an instruction that tells the agent to download, a Dockerfile that fetches at run time rather than build time, a solution that installs what it needs, or a test that reaches an endpoint are each a fail. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines are NOT part of this comparison: the judge needs egress to score the judged channel and the agent harness needs egress to reach its own model, neither of which is the task being online. An offline task with an online verifier and an online agent harness is the normal combination rather than a contradiction. Do not raise a finding merely because the bundle never mentions the network; silence is the expected state. If the work genuinely requires an outside endpoint, say so here as a design finding against the offline contract.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:57:- AL-24 Filename-keyed behaviour: could a "solution" skip the real work by keying on input filenames instead of content (a lookup table from names to memorized answers; rename the file and it breaks)? If the data layout makes this profitable, something in the tests must defeat it (e.g. eval inputs the agent never saw, content-based checks).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:58:- AL-25 Nondeterministic output: could an agent that guesses, races, or emits different output on each run luck into reward? Grading must be robust to this (enough independent inputs that guessing cannot win, or repeated-run identity where the instruction promises determinism).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:59:- AL-26 Metadata truthfulness: read instruction.md, decide for yourself what kind of work DOMINATES the task, then compare against the declared metadata. `[metadata] category` must name that dominant kind of work: a mismatch is a fail, and it is still a fail when the declared category appears only as a side aspect of the task (a Debugging task with a security flavour declared as "Security" fails). `[task] description` must be an honest summary of what the instruction asks: misleading = fail, sloppy but true = minor. `keywords` and `tags` must relate to the task's actual subject matter: completely unrelated entries are a fail, weak-but-related entries are a warn.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:61:## Task security (malicious-code audit)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:65:- AL-19 No credential or secret exfiltration: no credential shipped anywhere (including a literal value in `[verifier.env]`). Apart from the pinned `OPENAI_API_KEY` placeholder, nothing reads environment variables for credentials, ~/.ssh, ~/.aws, or any credential store, and nothing transmits or stages such values. Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:66:- AL-20 No network exfiltration or supply-chain risk: no curl/wget piped to a shell, no downloading and executing code from URLs, nothing that transmits bundle contents or grading data outward. The task runs offline, so there must be no outbound calls at all in the instruction, the Dockerfile's runtime path, the solution or the tests; AL-15 owns the plain "this cannot work offline" case, while what stays a fail here, and would stay one under any policy, is malicious use: exfiltration of secrets, bundle contents or grading data, and downloading then executing code. Fail. One exception: the official uv installer piped to a shell at build time in `environment/Dockerfile`, exactly `curl -LsSf https://astral.sh/uv/install.sh | sh`, as in the recommended DS-29 block. Any other piped download, or this one anywhere else (tests/Dockerfile, solve.sh, test.sh, the checks), still fails.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:67:- AL-21 No destructive or resource-abuse operations: no rm -rf outside the task's own working tree, no fork bombs, no disk-filling loops, no background daemons (a service a check starts for measurement and stops before the check returns is fine). Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:68:- AL-22 No obfuscated payloads: no base64/hex-encoded commands that get decoded and executed, no eval/exec of dynamically constructed strings, no invisible or deceptive Unicode. Encoding that is the task's own problem domain is fine when the instruction or TRUTH.md explains it. Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:69:- AL-23 No prompt injection in agent-visible files: nothing under environment/ or in instruction.md contains text that tries to steer the agent outside the task ("ignore previous instructions", instructions addressed to the model, hidden directives in comments or metadata). Fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:71:## Consistency sweep
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:73:- AL-16 Artifact filenames are identical across instruction, task.toml, solve.sh, and tests.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:74:- AL-17 Timeout story coherent: instruction's "You have N seconds" == agent timeout_sec; verifier timeout plausible for what test.sh does.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:75:- AL-18 No stale references: nothing mentions files, paths, counts, or numbers that do not match the actual bundle (e.g. docstring says 20 tests, file has 21 - fail; TRUTH.md describes input files environment/ does not contain - fail).
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:77:## Checklist (copy verbatim into your report)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/05_Alignment_Integrity_QC.md:124:## Severity guide
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:13:## Mindset
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:17:## Format rules (script-owned, here for context)
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:23:## Language rules
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:29:## Content rules
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:31:- IN-1 The sentence "You have N seconds to complete this task." (or a natural equivalent stating the exact number of seconds) is mandatory, and N must equal `[agent] timeout_sec` in task.toml.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:32:- IN-2 The instruction must never name anything under `tests/` or `solution/`: no test.sh, test_output.py, judge.toml, reward.toml, rewardkit, solve.sh, solve.py, no TRUTH.md, no "the grader" or "the checker", no `/logs/verifier`, no reward or CTRF file. TRUTH.md matters most of the set: it is the complete worked answer, so naming it tells the agent an answer document exists and invites a hunt for it (gate 02 DS-23). The bare word "cheat" is fine in prose (for example a sentence forbidding cheating) and is not scanned at all. The agent must not learn how it is scored from the prompt. The instruction MAY state WHAT the work will be judged on: the properties the deliverable must satisfy, expected output shape, edge cases it must handle, behaviour that must hold. It is equally free to point at a spec file that carries those details instead, and IN-8 treats that as complete. That is the acceptance surface, not the grading machinery. The line: describing requirements of the deliverable is fine; describing the mechanism that checks them (test names, weights, negative checks, judge prompts, reward files) is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:33:- IN-3 The instruction must never tell the agent to write a reward file or touch `/logs/`.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:34:- IN-4 No "benchmark" self-references, no GUIDs.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:35:- IN-5 The prompt owes nothing about the inputs, but a path it does name must exist. The prompt may leave the input files, their locations, their formats, their field and parameter names and every other input detail completely undescribed. An agent that has to open the container and look around to find what it is working with is doing ordinary work, not suffering a defect. Never raise a finding because the prompt did not mention an input, a path, or a detail of one. The single fail here is a wrong pointer: if the prompt does send the agent somewhere, that place must exist in the agent container. Three origins are all legitimate: `environment/`, which lands at `/app/data/...`; anything `environment/Dockerfile` puts there, whether copied in, generated by a `RUN` step or pulled from an earlier build stage, which is how most bundles ship config files, source trees and fixtures at paths like `/app/policy.yaml` or `/app/configs/`; and anything the agent itself creates. Open the Dockerfile before judging this, because a path outside `/app/data/` is normal and is not evidence of anything on its own. A path that exists in none of those three places misleads the agent and is a fail.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:36:- IN-6 The agent must be able to work out where each deliverable goes. The harness collects artifacts by copying from the exact absolute paths declared in `task.toml`, so an agent that writes perfect content in the wrong place scores nothing at all. The prompt must therefore either state the exact path, or describe it naturally enough that an intelligent reader lands on exactly one path and that path is the declared one. "Write the decoder to `/app/decode.py`" and "leave the finished decoder as `decode.py` in `/app`" are both fine. Wording that leaves two plausible landing places, or none, is a fail. In the other direction, the prompt must not ask for a deliverable that `task.toml` never declares, because nothing would collect it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:37:- IN-7 Every tool, binary, language or library the instruction tells the agent to use must be installed by `environment/Dockerfile` (deep check happens in Gate 02, but flag obvious misses here). This applies only to tools the prompt actually names; naming none is fine.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:38:- IN-8 Everything that grades the agent must be satisfiable by an agent holding only the prompt and the container. That means both layers, with one carve-out. Every expectation a test asserts, and every expectation an OUTCOME judged criterion demands (one that asserts something about what was produced), has to be knowable from something the agent can reach: the prompt, a specification or schema file shipped in `environment/`, or the shipped data read together with the prompt. Treat an unknowable outcome criterion exactly as seriously as an unknowable test; moving an expectation from one layer to the other does not make it fair. The carve-out is CONDUCT criteria, which judge how the agent worked rather than what it produced. Gate 04 JD-15 deliberately exempts them: a conduct criterion needs no sentence anywhere, because requiring one would force the working method into the prompt, which IN-14 forbids. Do not raise anything here about a criterion like "The agent verifies its encoder round-trips before concluding". It is judged by JD-15's two-step test, not by this one. What the prompt does NOT owe is restating any of it. A prompt that describes the job in a few natural paragraphs and points at a contract living elsewhere is complete. Silence about anything nothing grades is equally correct: the prompt is a job description, not a specification document, and missing detail that no test and no criterion checks is not a gap. Do not ask for more than the graded surface. The fail here is narrow: a test or an outcome criterion demands something (a key name, an exact string, an error shape, a precedence order, a numeric tolerance) that the agent could not have learned from any file it can see. Name the test or criterion, name the expectation, and say where you looked for it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:39:- IN-9 The instruction must not leak the answer or reference implementation: no worked example that IS the solution, no constants that the agent is supposed to discover, no spelled-out algorithm when discovering the algorithm is the task. This is a licence to withhold, not a licence to make the task unreachable. Whatever the instruction holds back must still be discoverable from the shipped data, so that a solver can test a guess and learn whether it was right. A rule withheld from the instruction AND unsupported by anything in environment/ is a solvability defect, not difficulty; gate 02 DS-21 and the foundational check F0e own that judgement, so note it here and let gate 02 rule on it.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:40:- IN-10 The "do not look up solutions online" sentence has NO check: its presence or absence is never reported or judged. Leave it alone either way.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:41:- IN-11 Network wording matches the offline contract. `[environment] network_mode` is pinned to `"no-network"` for every task, so there is no declared policy to look up and nothing to compare: the agent's machine has no network, full stop. The instruction must therefore never ask the agent to download, fetch, clone, install or browse anything, and must never name a URL as a source the agent is expected to read. Any such sentence is a fail, because the agent cannot comply and will burn its budget discovering that. The `[verifier]` and `[agent]` network lines are harness plumbing - the judge reaching its model and the agent harness reaching its own - and are irrelevant here; do not read them as the task being online. The instruction does NOT have to mention being offline: silence is correct and complete, and an instruction that never refers to the network is completely fine. If the task genuinely cannot be done without the network, that is a task-design finding against the offline contract, not a reason to treat the instruction as right.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:42:- IN-12 Metadata truthfulness: decide for yourself what kind of work DOMINATES the instruction, then compare against task.toml. `[metadata] category` must name that dominant kind of work: a mismatch is a fail, and it is still a fail when the declared category appears only as a side aspect of the task (a Debugging task with a security flavour declared as "Security" fails). `[task] description` must be an honest summary of what the instruction asks: misleading = fail, sloppy but true = minor. `keywords` and `tags` must relate to the task's actual subject matter: completely unrelated entries are a fail, weak-but-related entries are a warn.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:43:- IN-13 Internal consistency: the instruction must not contradict itself. Read every requirement against every other requirement. Two statements that demand incompatible things (one paragraph says "print one JSON object", another says "print one line per record"; a constraint forbids a tool another sentence requires), constraints that jointly make the task impossible to complete, or circular dependencies (A must be derived from B while B must be derived from A) are all fails. Name both sentences and explain why an agent cannot satisfy both at once.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:44:- IN-14 End state and rules of engagement, never the fix path. An instruction says two legitimate kinds of thing. It says WHAT the finished work must look like, and it says WHAT RULES the agent must work within while producing it: which files it may touch, which tool it must use, what order things happen in, what it must leave alone. What it must never do is hand over the method of solving. The discriminator, applied sentence by sentence: after reading this, does the agent know more about HOW TO GET the answer, or only about what it may and may not do while getting it? The first is a recipe. The second is a constraint, and constraints are expected here, not tolerated (gate 04 JD-16 grades them). Fine: natural context and partial direction ("start by reading the policy file", "the figures in your summary must agree with the ledger"), which orients the agent or states what the finished work must look like without telling it what it will find in the data, the line IN-17 draws; any stated rule of engagement ("change only the files under src/parser", "leave the config untouched", "use the vendored client rather than installing a fresh one", "get the migration written before you touch the schema"). Fail: a numbered or implied sequence of operations that, followed verbatim, produces the answer with no reasoning left ("open X, take the value in row 4, multiply by the rate in Y, write it to Z"); an exact formula handed over when deriving that formula is the work; naming the exact join keys or algorithm when discovering them is the difficulty the task claims. The test: could a careless agent score full reward by transcribing the instruction into code without understanding the problem? If yes, the difficulty is fake and this is a fail. A constraint never trips that test, because closing a door does not produce an answer.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:45:- IN-15 Every graded rule of engagement is stated somewhere the agent can read it. If a test or a judged criterion grades HOW the agent worked, the rule must be stated in a sentence the agent can read: the instruction itself, or any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. The prompt may delegate here just as it may anywhere else (IN-8), and need not name the file, so a task that says "the operating rules are in /app/data/RULES.md" is complete, and so is a RULES.md sitting in the input the agent was handed; you must not ask for the rules to be copied into the prompt. Open `tests/judge.toml` and list every criterion whose `[criterion.annotations]` table carries `type = "instruction following"`, which is the label a constraint criterion carries (gate 04 JD-16). Add any criterion you read as enforcing a rule of engagement whatever its label claims, since the label is a claim to check and not an answer to accept (JD-15), and leave out any you read as a standard of work nobody stated: verification habits, honest reporting and restraint are conduct criteria that need no sentence by design, because demanding one would force the method into the prompt and fail this gate under IN-14, so a conduct criterion wearing the `instruction following` label is a labelling defect for gate 04 rather than a missing sentence for you. Then scan `tests/test_output.py` for any check grading scope or what was left untouched. Tool-use and ordering rules will not appear among the tests, because tests may not read command history (gate 03 TS-12), so the judged channel is the only place they can be graded. For each one, find the sentence and name the file it lives in. A rule that is graded but appears in no file the agent can read costs the honest agent reward for something it had no way to know, which is the same defect as IN-8's unreachable expectation: fail, and name both the checker and the missing sentence. Vagueness counts against the author here, because a rule the agent cannot act on is not a stated rule. "Be careful around the config" states nothing; "do not modify anything under /etc" states a rule. Judge whether an agent could tell, before acting, whether a given action complies. This check runs in the same direction as gate 04 JD-16 and finds the same defect from the other side. That doubling is deliberate: each gate reads the rule from its own side, here from the prompt and there from the criterion, so a rule stated nowhere has to survive two readings instead of one. Reach your verdict here from the files, not from what the other gate concluded.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:46:- IN-16 No significant ambiguity in anything that is graded. IN-8 asks whether a graded expectation is reachable at all; this asks the narrower question of whether it is reachable in exactly one way. The defect has a precise shape, and you must show all three parts or you do not have a finding. First, something graded depends on the answer. Second, the contract admits two readings that a competent practitioner could both defend. Third, nothing in any file the agent can read picks between them, so the agent has to guess and the grader punishes half the guesses. Typical shapes: a boundary that could be inclusive or exclusive, an order that could be by first appearance or sorted, a total that could include or exclude a category, a field that could be omitted or emitted empty, a rounding or tie-break the contract never settles. Read the tests and the outcome judged criteria first, because they tell you which questions are actually graded, then read the contract asking whether it answers each one. Report it by writing out both readings in your own words, naming the checker that grades the answer, and saying which files you searched for the tiebreak. A finding without both readings written out is not usable and must not be raised. This is deliberately not a general complaint about vague prose. Wording you merely find loose, terse or unpolished is not this check. If both readings would satisfy every test and every criterion, there is no defect here, because nothing turns on the answer. Say nothing and move on.
Terminal_2.0_Harbor_Harness/Input_Bundle_QC/01_Instruction_QC.md:47:- IN-17 The instruction must not pre-announce what the agent is supposed to discover. Withholding is the default and difficulty is the point: traps, misleading surfaces, data whose shape only emerges from reading it, and rules that show themselves under a single condition are all legitimate, and gate 02 DS-21 protects them. What fails is a prompt that hands over the input's hidden structure, or the branches the work requires, before the agent has looked: "watch out for the null timestamps", "note that two records share an id", "some rows still use the old format", "remember the fiscal year starts in April". Each sentence like that converts a discovery into a transcription, and the bundle still grades clean afterwards, because every announced branch gets its fixture and AL-27 confirms the fixture fires, so nothing downstream will catch it and this gate is the only place it can be caught. Two conditions have to hold together, and the second is the one that separates a hint from a requirement. First, the sentence announces something the agent was meant to find for itself: a property of the input, an edge case inside it, a branch the work has to take. Second, the shipped input ALREADY establishes that thing, so the agent could have read it off the data it was handed (gate 05 AL-1 calls such a fact determinate and carries the worked cases to measure against, the sharpest being that an input which is merely silent about something has established nothing). Only when both hold is the sentence doing nothing but saving the agent the work. If the input does not establish it, the sentence is the only ground the graded item has, and deleting it would not make the task harder, it would make a graded item unfair (AL-1) or the task impossible (DS-21). That sentence is a requirement and it stays. Difficulty is not the test here and neither is how much the sentence gives away, which is why no rule of engagement is ever a hint: nothing in a data file can establish a prohibition, so "change only the files under src/parser" and "leave the config untouched" are requirements by construction, and IN-15 with gate 04 JD-16 oblige the author to state them. The same follows for a signpost that the bundle's own material is not authoritative (Final_checklist CL-6), for whatever settles a graded ambiguity (IN-16), and for the output's required shape and literals wherever the shipped input does not carry them itself (gate 02 DS-25). Default to keeping the sentence. To fail this check you must do two things: name the discovery the sentence replaces, and point to where the shipped input already establishes it. If you cannot do both, the prompt is being helpful rather than broken, and it passes. Orientation that discloses nothing is fine as well ("start by reading the policy file"), and so is any statement of what the finished work must look like. What this check removes is advance warning about what the agent will find once it starts reading.
