488:- [ ] CL-1 Every action the instruction demands is checked by something that looks at the action itself. List the actions the instruction asks for - start the service, call the endpoint, run the command, hit the API - and name what checks each one. Either channel counts: a test where the effect can still be inspected at grade time, or a judged criterion where the judge can see it in the trajectory. One or the other, not both, since the same observable graded twice is F0d/JD-8 overlap. What the checker must not do is settle for the agent's account of the action: a check that only reads a field the agent wrote proves nothing, because writing `"status": "ok"` costs nothing and a script that did no work at all can write it. Fail when an action has no checker, or when its only checker reads the agent's own summary of having done it. Scope it narrowly. This is for work that has an effect of its own, separate from the file the agent writes. Where the whole deliverable IS a computed artifact there is nothing else to look at and the check does not apply; whether that artifact could have been faked is CL-2 and CL-3. A client task shipped exactly this: the grader read only the agent's `report.json`, and a script that never started the service at all scored 1.000, byte-identical to the oracle.
489:- [ ] CL-2 Price the forgery before you accept the tests. Walk the positive tests and total what a deliberate fake earns: one hand-written artifact per declared path, filled with plausible values, produced by doing none of the work. Report that total as a share of the positive weight and name every test that paid it. A small share is normal and is not a defect by itself. Tests that pay for a parseable file, a valid shape or the deliverable simply existing are legitimate, and some tasks reasonably award a little for producing the right artifacts at all. Two things fail. One, the forgery reaching the range a genuine partial attempt would earn, because at that point the reward cannot distinguish faking from working. Two, any test that paid while believing it was grading content, which is a broken assertion regardless of the total. Say which of the two you found, or say the total is defensible and why. The number gets reported either way, because the value of this check is that somebody computed it: nobody did on the delivery the client audited, and outright fakes scored full marks on five separate tasks there.
490:- [ ] CL-3 The graded answer is earned, not copied. Take the values grading compares against - the tests, and any judged criterion that names a specific value - and ask whether they can be lifted straight out of something the agent already has: a field in a shipped file that happens to hold the answer, a constant sitting in the instruction, a worked example in the data. Deriving the answer is not this defect and never is - a task whose answer follows from processing the data is working exactly as intended, and CL-10 insists on that reachability. The defect is the answer being present rather than produced, so the tests cannot tell a solver from a transcriber. Fail, naming the file and field it can be copied from. In the client's finding the expected end state followed from constants plus one shipped plan file with no work in between.
491:- [ ] CL-4 Partial credit tracks partial correctness. Count the independently discoverable facts the deliverable carries (per field, per setting, per row), then read how the tests compare them. One wrong fact should cost about one fact's worth of reward. What fails is an answer that is almost entirely right scoring as though it were entirely wrong, and that happens two ways, both worth looking for: one whole-object equality settling N independent facts at once, and a pile of separately weighted tests that all key off that same single comparison. Report what an answer with exactly one fact wrong would score. Two things are not this defect. An instruction that states out loud that only an exact match counts has set that bar deliberately. And weight distribution by itself is fine: one test legitimately owning most of the reward because it checks the main outcome is good design, not a cliff, so do not go looking for a percentage. What matters is whether the curve can tell a near miss from a total miss. The client measured the failure: six of seven hidden settings correct scored 0.041, the same as none of them, because 14 tests worth +5 each all keyed off one dict comparison - 70 of 73 points turning on a single word.
492:- [ ] CL-5 No hidden conventions. Read the exact comparisons in the tests and ask what each one requires beyond the answer being right: the order of a list, the exact spelling of a key or a value, where the file goes and what it is called (`out.json` versus `/app/out/out.json`), letter casing. Each of those must either be stated in the instruction, or be established by an example the agent is actually given in the copied input, or be compared in a way that does not care. Anything else grades telepathy. Only what the assertion actually enforces counts. A test that parses JSON and compares dictionaries does not care about key order or whitespace, so there is nothing to find there; you are looking at what the comparison really demands, not at strictness it could theoretically have had. Read the judged criteria the same way: a criterion that expects a naming or layout the contract never states grades telepathy just as a test does. Fail, and quote both the assertion and the sentence that should have promised it. The client found eight such gates in one task, demanding the gold file's exact keys, values and list ordering with nothing in the instruction about ordering at all.
493:- [ ] CL-6 A wrong value we shipped ourselves must be signposted as suspect. Find every place the bundle hands the agent a value it could reasonably copy - sample code, a config, a template, an example payload - and check it against what grading demands, tests and judged criteria alike. Where the two differ, something the agent can read must mark our material as not authoritative. Almost anything does: the instruction saying the shipped code is broken or stale, the task being a repair or debugging task at all, a spec file named as the authority the code must be reconciled against, or a stated symptom the agent can reproduce. This check does not object to traps and does not require the bundle to be internally consistent; wrong values planted on purpose are often the whole task. What fails is the unsignposted version, where the shipped value looks exactly as authoritative as the truth and nothing the agent can read distinguishes them, so passing depends on distrusting our own material for no reason we ever gave. Fail, quoting both values. The client's case turned on one word: shipped sample code used `omit`, the hidden truth was `emit`, nothing flagged the sample as unreliable, and taking our own material at face value cost 70 of 73 points.
495:- [ ] CL-7 Every file the instruction names exists where grading happens. List each path `instruction.md` tells the agent to read, then confirm each one is present in every corpus the tests grade against, not merely somewhere in the bundle. A path that exists in the sample data but not in the graded data is a fail, and it is the worst kind of fail because following the instructions literally is what triggers it. The client's task told the agent to settle its parameters against a control file the graded corpus does not contain: 26 FileNotFoundErrors, reward 0.124, for doing exactly what the instruction said.
496:- [ ] CL-8 The reference solution does not survive by cheating. Read `solve.sh` and `solve.py` for any branch that skips a step the instruction requires: a try/except that falls back to hardcoded expected values, a default used when an input is missing, a shortcut around an unreachable resource. If the oracle needs that branch to score 1.000, the task is unsolvable as written and the oracle is hiding it. Fail, and quote the branch. In the client's finding our own solution silently fell back to hardcoded parameters, which no honest agent could have discovered and which the task's own anti-cheat test punishes.
497:- [ ] CL-9 Grading does not supply what it measures. Read `test.sh` and every check in `test_output.py` in execution order and sort what happens before the assertions into two kinds. Setup that makes measurement possible is normal and expected: copying the agent's artifacts into place, starting the app so a test can query it, creating the empty database an assertion will inspect. None of that is a finding. What fails is setup that produces the value being measured - a step that exercises a workflow and thereby creates the records a later test counts, or writes a file a later test reads as though the agent had written it. Fail, naming the step and the assertion, and say which of the two kinds you decided it was.
498:- [ ] CL-10 Solvable from `instruction.md` and `environment/` alone, given unlimited time and resources. Those two are everything the agent ever receives; `solution/` and `tests/` are not uploaded to it. So for every fact grading actually asserts - tests and judged criteria alike - name where the agent gets it: a file it can read, or a derivation from one. Derivation counts fully and is meant to - working the value out from the data is the task, and long or difficult work is not a defect here. Any fact that exists only in `solution/` or `tests/` is unreachable: a constant hardcoded in `solve.py`, a magic value living only in an assertion, a threshold only `test_output.py` knows, a convention only the gold file demonstrates. That part of the task cannot be solved with any amount of time or compute, because the information is not in the agent's world at all. Fail, and name the fact, the file it hides in, and the agent-visible file that should have carried it. F0e states the general principle; CL-10 is the specific way we have broken it, so run it as a file-by-file trace rather than a judgement call.
512:- [ ] CL-1..CL-10 client feedback checks all run, each one carrying the number it asks for or the file and line it asks for (never a bare ✅)

