master_chat_tasks/knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md:18:## 0. Why our levers failed (read once)
master_chat_tasks/knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md:23:[v3: our 24-26 Oct builds, registry/tasks.json] Our 8 parked builds 24-26 Oct were small, fully stated contracts with a computational core. Blind Opus sessions transliterated them into exact programs in about 3 minutes (cleanroom 3/3 at 98/98, crash-recovery 3/3 at 95/95, batch-lease 3/3 at 139/139), and stated limits handed over the fast exact method (cold-chain probe tests 100, final 85.0; flood: 2 of 3 found the DP in about 5 minutes). Exact optimum with a stated method is easy for Opus (g4/C_80877080.md:9: solved exactly on 9 corpora in 24 minutes).
master_chat_tasks/knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md:27:[v3: g1 GS:5-10, g2 GS:23, g3 GS:13] 26 Oct, from 40 delivered tasks: delivered peers stay below a final of 70 in three ways. (a) Breadth: 30 to 80 plainly stated rules over many files and formats, graded whole-family exact. (b) A degenerate shipped instance: the rule space is stated, the hard branch fires only on generated hidden corpora, and most test weight sits there. (c) A judge half built as a probe band of acts Opus skips. Section 1A ranks the sources; section 4 builds the judge; section 8 lists the peer losses we must never copy.
master_chat_tasks/knowledge/LEVER_RULES_v3.md:29:## 0b. The determinate test (05:21-32; also decides IN-17, TS-19, JD-15)
