    18	## 0. Why our levers failed (read once)
    19	Every rule sat in one plain sentence near its use, sometimes with the trap explained. claude-opus-5 reads every spec in its first minute, writes its program once 3 to 10 minutes in, and never changes the graded logic afterwards. It self-tests on shipped data, on its own tiny examples, and on invariants (totals, determinism). It transliterates ANY explicit rule sentence, not only full procedures, and its self-check reproduces its own reading, so a control that reconciles only under the right reading confirms the rule for it. Known leaks: `knowledge/LEAK_LIBRARY.md`.
    20	
    21	[v3: large specs behave differently, g4/C_8764bfdd.md:91, g4/C_4e2f6925.md:17, g3/C_56fe3bdd.md:19-21] With 30 or more rules over many files Opus reads for 4 to 11 minutes, makes its first full run at 10 to 46 minutes, and keeps editing graded logic for up to 90 minutes. It still loses points: on rules it read once and never re-read, on branches the shipped data never fires, and on checks it never ran.
    22	
    23	[v3: our 24-26 Oct builds, registry/tasks.json] Our 8 parked builds 24-26 Oct were small, fully stated contracts with a computational core. Blind Opus sessions transliterated them into exact programs in about 3 minutes (cleanroom 3/3 at 98/98, crash-recovery 3/3 at 95/95, batch-lease 3/3 at 139/139), and stated limits handed over the fast exact method (cold-chain probe tests 100, final 85.0; flood: 2 of 3 found the DP in about 5 minutes). Exact optimum with a stated method is easy for Opus (g4/C_80877080.md:9: solved exactly on 9 corpora in 24 minutes).
    24	
    25	25 Oct: the peer losses we envied came mostly from silence (an unstated binding time, an open operand, a second model that also fits), which QC still forbids (05:27). The legal difficulty left is (a) composition and amplification of stated rules on hidden inputs and (b) rules with no sentence that one determinate shipped fact settles (L11). Withholding is the kit's default (01:47 "Withholding is the default and difficulty is the point").
