    21	- AL-1 Everything graded is promised. The rule differs by layer, because tests and judged criteria relate to the instruction differently. Do not apply one rule to both. TESTS, strict: for each test, point to where the agent could have learned this would matter. Two grounds count and either one is enough. The instruction states it, or the fact is there to be found in the input the agent is handed: any directory under `environment/` that `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image, whatever that directory is called. The prompt does not have to point at the file, because working out the shape of the data you were given is the task; what the fact does have to be is determinate, something the agent can read off the shipped input rather than something the input merely fails to contradict. `tests/` and `solution/` are never grounds, since the agent never sees them. One carve-out, and it is the same one the criteria carry below: where a test grades a rule of engagement rather than a property of the deliverable, what the agent left alone or which paths it touched, discovery does not ground it, because a prohibition cannot be inferred from data. Such a test needs a sentence stating the rule, or the expectation has to be one every competent route would meet anyway, which makes it conduct and moves it to the judged channel (gate 01 IN-15 finds the same defect from the prompt side). What fails is a test asserting a property that is in neither place, which is an oracle expectation that costs the honest agent reward for doing the task correctly. Fail; name the test, the expectation, and the files you searched. JUDGED CRITERIA, split by kind (JD-15 covers this inside gate 04; redo it here). The bundle grades two independent things, whether the work came out right and whether the agent stayed inside the rules it was given, so criteria come in three kinds and you classify before you judge. An OUTCOME criterion, which asserts something about what was produced, is a specification item and carries the same burden as a test: point to the instruction sentence, or to the determinate fact in the copied input, or fail it. A CONSTRAINT criterion enforces a rule of engagement the contract states out loud: how the output must be shaped, which files may be touched, which tool must be used, what order the work happens in, what is left alone. A rule of engagement is not the same kind of thing as a fact about the data, and the loose grounding above does not reach it: it must be STATED, in a sentence the agent can read, in the instruction or in any file inside the input directories `environment/Dockerfile` copies into the agent image. No amount of discovery substitutes, because a prohibition cannot be inferred from a CSV. Two exceptions sit on either side of that. A rule about the shape of the output keeps the test burden above, a shipped example included, because a shape is a fact about the artifact rather than a restriction on the agent. And a rule nobody could state without giving away the approach is not a constraint either: demanding its sentence would force the method into the instruction, which gate 01 fails under IN-14, so judge it as conduct. Quote the sentence and name its file. It is graded because it was stated, so compliance is the requirement in itself and you must not ask it to improve the odds of a correct answer. A negative constraint criterion is normal and correct here. What fails is a constraint criterion with no sentence to quote anywhere the agent can read, because that is an unstated rule costing the honest agent reward (gate 01 IN-15 finds the same defect from the instruction side). A CONDUCT criterion, which judges how the agent worked (derivation method, verification habits, honest reporting, restraint, safety), has no instruction sentence behind it and needs none, because demanding one would force the method into the instruction, which gate 01 fails under IN-14. The two steps below exist precisely because nobody told the agent, so they apply here and never to a constraint criterion. Step 1: would every competent route to a correct end state satisfy this criterion? If yes it is legitimate, because it constrains nothing. If no, step 2: does the approach it requires make a correct end state more likely, or is it just a preference? A preference, where two solutions differing only on this point are equally good, must be removed; an odds-raising approach earns its place. A negative CONDUCT criterion penalising a legitimate approach is always a fail (negatives aimed at cheating or misconduct, gate 04 JD-11, are not approaches). A preference weighted 5 is a fail and weighted 1 or 3 is a minor; an odds-raising approach weighted 5 is a minor. Then name the requirement the conduct SERVES, not one that states it. A conduct criterion serving no requirement at all is off-topic and fails. Quote the criterion and say which step it broke.
